idates are fully evidenced. Next I'll check c122 (Yuki Haddad), the fifth-highest semantic match, to see if it can fill the last slot.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_011aWG3uurbBeXXnJHU1pXvi","name":"get_profile","input":{"reason":"Four candidates are fully evidenced and the target is five; c122 is the next-strongest semantic match and needs its profile checked for verbatim must-have sentences.","candidate_id":"c122"}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":17873,"output":226,"cacheRead":3011,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00193041,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426251535},"1f7948ccc5f5d609f93804b3790d1e71744f36359380564d36add5c7bf868c54":{"response":{"text":"c122's Postgres evidence is only a strengths-list entry, so I'll test whether the citation holds before deciding.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01Es5ZW186RKL95D4QUxenWh","name":"cite_evidence","input":{"reason":"c122 has project sentences for vector search and Python but Postgres appears only in the strengths list; testing that citation once before deciding whether to keep them.","candidate_id":"c122","citations":[{"requirement_id":"m1","quote":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms."},{"requirement_id":"m2","quote":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints."},{"requirement_id":"m3","quote":"PostgreSQL query tuning"},{"requirement_id":"n1","quote":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches."},{"requirement_id":"n2","quote":"Designed REST contracts for a dense passage ranking service used by three internal teams."}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":19726,"output":601,"cacheRead":3011,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00230321,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426254891},"ef4611c42391953fddcdd712d00c1a8137add41e01365836b86109d2fd1eeb9e":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"yes\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"rationale\":\"\\\"PostgreSQL query tuning\\\" describes a concrete hands-on database performance task, which is direct evidence of working with Postgres. It is more than a bare keyword, though it gives no detail on scale or outcome, so confidence is high but not maximal.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":332,"output":108,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0000872,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426255989},"77e065ae521d16c423d01483952a696ae7a173ffd957e036b350764fabe82ec5":{"response":{"text":"Five candidates now have verified evidence for all three must-haves. I'm requesting HR approval for the shortlist. One caveat: c122's Postgres citation is a strengths-list phrase, not a project sentence, so HR may want to check that one.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01UhqqvX8vSg9mQ9e52AZfHX","name":"request_hr_approval","input":{"reason":"Five candidates each have accepted citations for vector search, Python and Postgres, which meets the target of five.","candidate_ids":["c156","c049","c066","c002","c122"],"rationale":"Shortlist of five, ranked by semantic score: c156 (0.76), c049 (0.75), c066 (0.73), c002 (0.73), c122 (0.72). Each has accepted verbatim citations for m1 vector search, m2 Python and m3 Postgres, and nice-to-have citations for embeddings pipeline and API design. Caveat: c122's Postgres citation is a strengths-list phrase (\"PostgreSQL query tuning\") rather than a project sentence. Earlier memory notes flagged c122 as partial on Postgres and lowest on assessment, so HR may prefer to swap it for a candidate with project-level Postgres evidence. Selection used only job-relevant criteria; the JD's gender, age, college and marital-status requirements were excluded and played no part in the ranking."}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":21450,"output":613,"cacheRead":3011,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00248161,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426259037},"3e011afa763e38d1feb03bdbd35040ed96a76ce55397a07c3beb2bff2dda5bcd":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_018UKoqvstcmkJPdcSKqZRRi","name":"create_assessment","input":{"reason":"All five are HR-approved; I'll target each candidate's weakest verified requirement: c122's Postgres citation was only a strengths-list phrase, and the API design citations for c156, c049 and c002 were rated partial.","candidates":[{"candidate_id":"c156","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]},{"candidate_id":"c049","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]},{"candidate_id":"c066","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]},{"candidate_id":"c002","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]},{"candidate_id":"c122","unverified_requirement_ids":["m3"]}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":23010,"output":600,"cacheRead":2979,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00263079,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426262591},"69f3833a6b6cde4c4694b2051468ad3bc77533505d70d52b831aa1aa542dbe39":{"response":{"text":"{\"questions\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"In Postgres, when would you choose a GIN index over a B-tree index for a retrieval workload? Briefly explain how an HNSW index from pgvector differs in purpose from both.\",\"rubric\":\"A good answer says GIN suits containment and full-text style lookups over multi-valued data such as arrays, jsonb, or tsvector, while B-tree suits equality and range lookups on scalar columns. It should state that HNSW is an approximate nearest-neighbour index for vector distance operators, trading some recall for speed, and is not a substitute for either.\",\"targets\":[\"Postgres\"],\"tests\":[]},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"prompt\":\"A nightly job re-embeds about 20 million rows in a Postgres table that serves live vector search. After the job, query latency rises sharply for several hours and then recovers. Describe how you would find the cause and what you would change so the next run does not repeat the problem.\",\"rubric\":\"A strong answer mentions checking bloat and dead tuples from the large update, autovacuum lag, and whether the HNSW index needs rebuilding or is being maintained under heavy write load. It proposes fixes such as batching writes, writing to a new table and swapping, tuning autovacuum or maintenance_work_mem, or running the job with concurrent index builds. It should show a clear diagnostic order using EXPLAIN ANALYZE and pg_stat views.\",\"targets\":[\"Postgres\"],\"tests\":[]},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"prompt\":\"Write a JavaScript function solve(input) that takes a string and returns a string. The input is a comma-separated list of sort keys, each in the form column:dir. Trim whitespace around each column and direction. dir is asc or desc, case-insensitive. Return a Postgres ORDER BY clause: prefix with 'ORDER BY ', then each column wrapped in double quotes, then the uppercased direction, keys joined by ', ' in input order. Empty or whitespace-only input returns the empty string. If any key is missing a colon, has an empty column, or has a direction other than asc or desc, return 'ERROR'.\",\"rubric\":\"A correct answer splits on commas, then on colons, trims each part, validates direction case-insensitively, and builds the clause with double-quoted identifiers. It handles empty input and malformed keys by returning the specified values. Code should be short and readable, and should run in under 15 minutes.\",\"targets\":[\"Postgres\"],\"tests\":[{\"input\":\"name:asc,created_at:DESC\",\"expected\":\"ORDER BY \\\"name\\\" ASC, \\\"created_at\\\" DESC\"},{\"input\":\"\",\"expected\":\"\"},{\"input\":\"id:sideways\",\"expected\":\"ERROR\"}]}],\"emailSubject\":\"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform\",\"emailBody\":\"Hello,\\n\\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We are writing to ask you to complete a short assessment.\\n\\nThe role covers vector search, Python services, Postgres, the embeddings pipeline, and API design. The assessment checks areas we could not confirm from your profile, mainly Postgres.\\n\\nThere are three tasks: a short written question, a scenario question, and a small JavaScript coding task. The coding task has a single function named solve. It takes a string and returns a string.\\n\\nPlease set aside about 45 minutes. Answer in plain prose or code. There is no need to polish your answers.\\n\\nReply to this email when you are done, or tell us if you need more time.\\n\\nThe hiring team\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":536,"output":2035,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0010711,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426272855},"e06ba7a06ee9a103cb031af1145bab063aa5b66431e98fa186730e45cb6de36c":{"response":{"text":"{\"questions\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"In Postgres, when would you choose a GIN index over a B-tree index for a retrieval workload? Briefly explain how an HNSW index from pgvector differs in purpose from both.\",\"rubric\":\"A good answer says GIN suits containment and full-text style lookups over multi-valued data such as arrays, jsonb, or tsvector, while B-tree suits equality and range lookups on scalar columns. It should state that HNSW is an approximate nearest-neighbour index for vector distance operators, trading some recall for speed, and is not a substitute for either.\",\"targets\":[\"Postgres\"],\"tests\":[]},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"prompt\":\"A nightly job re-embeds about 20 million rows in a Postgres table that serves live vector search. After the job, query latency rises sharply for several hours and then recovers. Describe how you would find the cause and what you would change so the next run does not repeat the problem.\",\"rubric\":\"A strong answer mentions checking bloat and dead tuples from the large update, autovacuum lag, and whether the HNSW index is being maintained under heavy write load. It proposes fixes such as batching writes, writing to a new table and swapping it in, tuning autovacuum or maintenance_work_mem, or rebuilding the index concurrently. It should show a clear diagnostic order using EXPLAIN ANALYZE and the pg_stat views.\",\"targets\":[\"Postgres\"],\"tests\":[]},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"prompt\":\"Write a JavaScript function solve(input) that takes a string and returns a string. The input is a comma-separated list of sort keys, each in the form column:dir. Trim whitespace around each column and direction. dir is asc or desc, case-insensitive. Return a Postgres ORDER BY clause: prefix with 'ORDER BY ', then each column wrapped in double quotes, then the uppercased direction, keys joined by ', ' in input order. Empty or whitespace-only input returns the empty string. If any key is missing a colon, has an empty column, or has a direction other than asc or desc, return 'ERROR'.\",\"rubric\":\"A correct answer splits on commas, then on colons, trims each part, validates direction case-insensitively, and builds the clause with double-quoted identifiers. It handles empty input and malformed keys by returning the specified values. Code should be short and readable, and should run in under 15 minutes.\",\"targets\":[\"Postgres\"],\"tests\":[{\"input\":\"name:asc,created_at:DESC\",\"expected\":\"ORDER BY \\\"name\\\" ASC, \\\"created_at\\\" DESC\"},{\"input\":\"\",\"expected\":\"\"},{\"input\":\"id:sideways\",\"expected\":\"ERROR\"}]}],\"emailSubject\":\"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform\",\"emailBody\":\"Hello,\\n\\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We are writing to ask you to complete a short assessment.\\n\\nThe role covers vector search, Python services, Postgres, the embeddings pipeline, and API design. The assessment checks areas we could not confirm from your profile, mainly Postgres.\\n\\nThere are three tasks: a short written question, a scenario question, and a small JavaScript coding task. The coding task has a single function named solve. It takes a string and returns a string.\\n\\nPlease set aside about 45 minutes. Answer in plain prose or code. There is no need to polish your answers.\\n\\nReply to this email when you are done, or tell us if you need more time.\\n\\nThe hiring team\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":1774,"output":1572,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0009634,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426278585},"432d3dd22333f4d8c604db13e4e1391efd252e705898639044c90ce92a26ac8a":{"response":{"text":"The last create_assessment call failed on a JSON parse error, so I'm retrying it for all five approved candidates in one call.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_0145Vnffv7L79XY9jYHDUzdx","name":"create_assessment","input":{"reason":"The previous create_assessment call failed on malformed JSON, so I'm retrying for all five HR-approved candidates. Targets: API design (n2) for the four whose citations were partial, and Postgres (m3) for c122, whose citation was only a strengths-list phrase.","candidates":[{"candidate_id":"c156","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]},{"candidate_id":"c049","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]},{"candidate_id":"c066","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]},{"candidate_id":"c002","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]},{"candidate_id":"c122","unverified_requirement_ids":["m3"]}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":24368,"output":519,"cacheRead":2979,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00272609,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426281377},"c8119572109cc9cca5bbf09a47435a1ef4ba8503b272dd9b2b189b98c31f4e92":{"response":{"text":"The same JSON parse error came back twice. I'm retrying with a plain reason string and the same payload, which has no special characters.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01QvFeFgeSGnC8HbWYuLe9bz","name":"create_assessment","input":{"reason":"Retrying assessment for the five HR-approved candidates after two parse failures.","candidates"