me":"get_profile","input":{"candidate_id":"c118","reason":"Four of five fully evidenced; c118 (0.70) has a PySpark/Kafka/DAG profile similar to the verified set, so check for the fifth slot."}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":20151,"output":295,"cacheRead":3011,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00219271,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426575668},"794a3fdd11c5e147ce793f6712cd48fc4a74f27b3b9b9faf8cb3f5add7fdcc2b":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_014Ygj2UzXdiTLGp4GjyZSjH","name":"cite_evidence","input":{"reason":"Pranav Saxena profile has explicit PySpark, SQL and Kafka pipeline sentences covering the must-haves, plus DAG orchestration and streaming.","candidate_id":"c118","citations":[{"requirement_id":"m1","quote":"Rebuilt nightly PySpark jobs on Databricks and cut the batch window from 6 hours to 50 minutes."},{"requirement_id":"m2","quote":"Wrote the SQL that reconciles finance and product marts, with window functions and tested dbt models."},{"requirement_id":"m3","quote":"Wrote Kafka consumers that land clickstream events in BigQuery within a minute."},{"requirement_id":"n2","quote":"Wrote Kafka consumers that land clickstream events in BigQuery within a minute."}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":21999,"output":362,"cacheRead":3011,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0024110100000000003,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426577401},"37656e2fd6e4b06e57653e1895f203fd0229600a12aafe44b56db8a2aae795b1":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"yes\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"rationale\":\"The sentence describes building Kafka consumers that ingest clickstream events and load them into BigQuery with a stated latency, which is concrete, hands-on data pipeline work.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":351,"output":81,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0000756,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426578530},"3ea7bb56f404c081586c5167158cf2ba14e4dca2678a2e4f600aacc1e11762e0":{"response":{"text":"Five candidates now have verified evidence for all three must-haves, which meets the target of five. I'm requesting HR approval for the shortlist.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01LE41LLS7118BttELyW5FxZ","name":"request_hr_approval","input":{"reason":"Target of 5 reached: five candidates each have verbatim evidence for Spark, SQL and data pipelines; one candidate was dropped for keyword-only claims.","candidate_ids":["c030","c041","c171","c051","c118"],"rationale":"Shortlist of five, all with cited evidence for m1 Spark, m2 SQL, m3 data pipelines. Nice-to-haves: Airflow (DAG orchestration) and streaming (Kafka consumers) evidenced for most. Dropped c177 (Jay Moreau): keyword-only skill list with QA-automation projects and 1 year of experience. Reformulated search once; 2 reformulations remain unused."}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":23543,"output":361,"cacheRead":3011,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00256491,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426580757},"b53e04a145596b7e02be151c547808abd71d6f18ceda0359f7d7e8de99f8b114":{"response":{"text":"Five candidates are approved, and each has a gap I couldn't verify from the profile. I'll generate targeted assessments for those gaps.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_017BYCdYLn5ia1252K3AUEHd","name":"create_assessment","input":{"reason":"Approved shortlist has verified must-haves; questions target the unverified gaps: Airflow for c030 and c118, SQL depth for c041, streaming for c171 and c051.","candidates":[{"candidate_id":"c030","unverified_requirement_ids":["n1"]},{"candidate_id":"c041","unverified_requirement_ids":["m2"]},{"candidate_id":"c171","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]},{"candidate_id":"c051","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]},{"candidate_id":"c118","unverified_requirement_ids":["n1"]}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":24909,"output":639,"cacheRead":2979,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0028401900000000002,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426584660},"c86310252d92c37953514d121b7c97f5dcce01d4aaa940190bc43aa6708e0d7f":{"response":{"text":"{\"questions\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"In Airflow, what is the difference between a task's retries and a DAG run's catchup setting? Give one example of when you would set catchup to False.\",\"rubric\":\"A strong answer says retries re-run a failed task instance within the same DAG run, while catchup controls whether the scheduler creates runs for past schedule intervals that have not yet run. It should give a concrete case, such as a new DAG with a start date far in the past where only current runs are wanted, or a pipeline whose historical loads would be expensive or wrong to replay.\",\"targets\":[\"Airflow\"],\"tests\":[]},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"prompt\":\"A daily Airflow DAG loads 200 partitions into a warehouse table. The task fails after 120 partitions have loaded. Describe how you would make the rerun safe and what changes you would make to the DAG so the same failure does not cause duplicate or missing data next time.\",\"rubric\":\"A good answer makes each partition write idempotent, for example by overwriting or merging by partition key instead of appending, and mentions using the logical date or data interval in the task rather than wall-clock time. It should also cover setting retries and clear task-level boundaries, and naming how the team would verify row counts after a rerun.\",\"targets\":[\"Airflow\"],\"tests\":[]},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"prompt\":\"Write a JavaScript function solve(input) that takes a string of task dependencies and returns the execution order as a string.\\n\\nInput: comma-separated edges in the form \\\"a>b\\\", meaning task a must run before task b. An empty string means no tasks.\\n\\nOutput: all task names in a valid topological order, separated by single spaces. When several tasks are ready at once, pick the alphabetically smallest first. If the dependencies contain a cycle, return \\\"cycle\\\".\\n\\nExamples:\\n\\\"extract>transform,transform>load\\\" returns \\\"extract transform load\\\"\\n\\\"a>b,b>a\\\" returns \\\"cycle\\\"\\n\\\"b>c,a>c\\\" returns \\\"a b c\\\"\",\"rubric\":\"A correct solution builds an in-degree map and uses Kahn's algorithm with a sorted ready list, or an equivalent approach that always picks the smallest available node. It must detect cycles by checking whether all nodes were emitted. Most candidates should finish within 15 minutes.\",\"targets\":[\"Airflow\"],\"tests\":[{\"input\":\"extract>transform,transform>load\",\"expected\":\"extract transform load\"},{\"input\":\"a>b,b>a\",\"expected\":\"cycle\"},{\"input\":\"b>c,a>c\",\"expected\":\"a b c\"}]}],\"emailSubject\":\"Data Engineer (mid): take-home assessment\",\"emailBody\":\"Hello,\\n\\nThank you for applying for the Data Engineer (mid) role. We are writing to ask you to complete a short assessment.\\n\\nThe assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript coding task. The scenario and the short answer are about how you would design and run data pipelines. The coding task is a single function with a few test inputs.\\n\\nPlease set aside about 45 minutes. You do not need to finish in one sitting, but please return everything within three days of receiving this email.\\n\\nReply to this email with your answers and the code. If anything in the instructions is unclear, ask us before you start.\\n\\nThe hiring team\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":522,"output":1529,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0008167000000000001,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426592350},"0d08f782a13093daf196ef81c8627ec2e11d267c501b8ddd8387099bc17615e1":{"response":{"text":"{\"questions\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"In Airflow, what is the difference between a task's retries and a DAG's catchup setting? Give one example of when you would set catchup to False.\",\"rubric\":\"A strong answer says retries re-run a failed task instance within the same DAG run, while catchup controls whether the scheduler creates runs for past schedule intervals that have not yet run. It should give a concrete case, such as a new DAG with a start date far in the past where only current runs are wanted, or a pipeline whose historical loads would be expensive or wrong to replay.\",\"targets\":[\"Airflow\"],\"tests\":[]},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"prompt\":\"A daily Airflow DAG loads 200 partitions into a warehouse table. The task fails after 120 partitions have loaded. Describe how you would make the rerun safe and what changes you would make to the DAG so the same failure does not cause duplicate or missing data next time.\",\"rubric\":\"A good answer makes each partition write idempotent, for example by overwriting or merging by partition key instead of appending, and mentions using the logical date or data interval in the task rather than wall-clock time. It should also cover setting retries and clear task-level boundaries, and name how the team would verify row counts after a rerun.\",\"targets\":[\"Airflow\"],\"tests\":[]},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"prompt\":\"Write a JavaScript function solve(input) that takes a string of task dependencies and returns the execution order as a string.\\n\\nInput: comma-separated edges in the form a>b, meaning task a must run before task b. An empty string means no tasks.\\n\\nOutput: all task names in a valid topological order, separated by single spaces. When several tasks are ready at once, pick the alphabetically smallest first. If the dependencies contain a cycle, return cycle.\\n\\nExamples:\\nextract>transform,transform>load returns extract transform load\\na>b,b>a returns cycle\\nb>c,a>c returns a b c\",\"rubric\":\"A correct solution builds an in-degree map and uses Kahn's algorithm with a sorted ready list, or an equivalent approach that always picks the smallest available node. It must detect cycles by checking whether all nodes were emitted. Most candidates should finish within 15 minutes.\",\"targets\":[\"Airflow\"],\"tests\":[{\"input\":\"extract>transform,transform>load\",\"expected\":\"extract transform load\"},{\"input\":\"a>b,b>a\",\"expected\":\"cycle\"},{\"input\":\"b>c,a>c\",\"expected\":\"a b c\"}]}],\"emailSubject\":\"Data Engineer (mid): take-home assessment\",\"emailBody\":\"Hello,\\n\\nThank you for applying for the Data Engineer (mid) role. We are writing to ask you to complete a short assessment.\\n\\nThe assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript coding task. The scenario and the short answer are about how you would design and run data pipelines. The coding task is a single function with a few test inputs.\\n\\nPlease set aside about 45 minutes. You do not need to finish in one sitting, but please return everything within three days of receiving this email.\\n\\nReply to this email with your answers and the code. If anything in the instructions is unclear, ask us before you start.\\n\\nThe hiring team\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":1677,"output":1633,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0009842,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426598305},"4fc4f5060944fbe362ad14e56c14c376b81cd01aec8e094c1d26a3537192527d":{"response":{"text":"{\"questions\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"Explain the difference between WHERE and HAVING in SQL. Then give one example where you would need HAVING and WHERE could not do the job.\",\"rubric\":\"A good answer says WHERE filters individual rows before grouping and HAVING filters groups after aggregation. The example should use an aggregate such as COUNT or SUM in the condition, for instance keeping only customers with more than five orders.\",\"targets\":[\"SQL\"],\"tests\":[]},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"prompt\":\"A nightly job joins an orders table to a customers table and writes the result to a reporting table. After a recent change, total revenue in the report is higher than the source system. Describe how you would find the cause and how you would fix the query so this does not recur.\",\"rubric\":\"A strong answer suspects a join producing duplicate rows, for example a non-unique key on the customers side, and checks row counts before and after the join. The fix should enforce key uniqueness, deduplicate the lookup side explicitly, or add a test that compares aggregates to the source.\",\"targets\":[\"SQL\"],\"tests\":[]},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"prompt\":\"Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string. Each line has the form user,amount where amount is an integer that may be negative. Ignore empty lines. Sum the amounts per user. Return one line per user in the form user,total, sorted by user name ascending, with lines joined by a newline and no trailing newline. If there are no valid lines, return an empty string.\",\"rubric\":\"The solution should split on newlines, skip blank lines, parse amounts as integers, and accumulate totals in a map. It should sort keys with a plain string comparison and join the results. Careless handling of empty input or trailing newlines is the most likely failure.\",\"targets\":[\"SQL\"],\"tests\":[{\"input\":\"a,1\\nb,2\\na,3\",\"expected\":\"a,4\\nb,2\"},{\"input\":\"\",\"expected\":\"\"},{\"input\":\"z,5\\ny,-2\\nz,1\\n\",\"expected\":\"y,-2\\nz,6\"}]}],\"emailSubject\":\"Data Engineer (mid) assessment\",\"emailBody\":\"Hello,\\n\\nThank you for applying for the Data Engineer (mid) role. We would like you to complete a short assessment before we move forward.\\n\\nThere are three tasks. One is a short written question about SQL. One is a short scenario about diagnosing a data problem. The last is a small JavaScript exercise. Write your answers in plain text or a code file.\\n\\nPlease allow about 45 minutes. You do not need to finish in one sitting, but please return it within five days.\\n\\nReply to this email with your answers attached or pasted below.\\n\\nThe hiring team\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":521,"output":1428,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0007661,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426605908},"3c8c95a433f4ac2e446294b6e23edec6d0de12f79a1aec8c25f7d53d4914861d":{"response":{"text":"{\"questions\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"Explain the difference between WHERE and HAVING in SQL. Then give one example where you would need HAVING and WHERE could not do the job.\",\"rubric\":\"A good answer says WHERE filters individual rows before grouping and HAVING filters groups after aggregation. The example should use an aggregate such as COUNT or SUM in the condition, for instance keeping only customers with more than five orders.\",\"targets\":[\"SQL\"],\"tests\":[]},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"prompt\":\"A nightly job joins an orders table to a customers table and writes the result to a reporting table. After a recent change, total revenue in the report is higher than the source system. Describe how you would find the cause and how you would fix the query so this does not recur.\",\"rubric\":\"A strong answer suspects a join producing duplicate rows, for example a non-unique key on the customers side, and checks row counts before and after the join. The fix should enforce key uniqueness, deduplicate the lookup side explicitly, or add a test that compares aggregates to the source.\",\"targets\":[\"SQL\"],\"tests\":[]},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"prompt\":\"Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string. Each line has the form user,amount where amount is an integer that may be negative. Ignore empty lines. Sum the amounts per user. Return one line per user in the form user,total, sorted by user name ascending, with lines joined by a newline and no trailing newline. If there are no valid lines, return an empty string.\",\"rubric\":\"The solution should split on newlines, skip blank lines, parse amounts as integers, and accumulate totals in a map. It should sort keys with a plain string comparison and join the results. Careless handling of empty input or trailing newlines is the most likely failure.\",\"targets\":[\"SQL\"],\"tests\":[{\"input\":\"a,1\\nb,2\\na,3\",\"expected\":\"a,4\\nb,2\"},{\"input\":\"\",\"expected\":\"\"},{\"input\":\"z,5\\ny,-2\\nz,1\\n\",\"expected\":\"y,-2\\nz,6\"}]}],\"emailSubject\":\"Data Engineer (mid) assessment\",\"emailBody\":\"Hello,\\n\\nThank you for applying for the Data Engineer (mid) role. We would like you to complete a short assessment before we move forward.\\n\\nThere are three tasks. One is a short written question about SQL. One is a short scenario about diagnosing a data problem. The last is a small JavaScript exercise. Write your answers in plain text or a code file.\\n\\nPlease allow about 45 minutes. You do not need to finish in one sitting, but please return it within five days.\\n\\nReply to this email with your answers attached or pasted below.\\n\\nThe hiring team\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":1458,"output":1356,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0008238,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426611085},"f5698fedcfb26c4532845b58b28f0a546251916ad16ecabb9ddbce586d3c35ca":{"response":{"text":"{\"questions\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"In a streaming pipeline, what is the difference between at-least-once and exactly-once delivery? Give one concrete way you would make a sink safe against duplicate records when the source can replay.\",\"rubric\":\"A good answer says at-least-once can redeliver events after a failure, so duplicates are possible, while exactly-once is an end-to-end property that needs checkpointing plus an idempotent or transactional sink. The concrete fix should be specific, such as upserting on a business key, using a unique event id with a merge, or writing with transactional commits tied to offsets.\",\"targets\":[\"Streaming\"],\"tests\":[]},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"prompt\":\"You consume clickstream events from Kafka with Spark Structured Streaming and write hourly counts per page to a warehouse table. Mobile clients sometimes send events up to two hours late. Describe how you would set up windowing and watermarking so the counts are correct, what you would do about events that arrive after the watermark, and how you would check the output against a batch recount.\",\"rubric\":\"A strong answer uses event-time tumbling windows with a watermark sized to the expected lateness, and explains the tradeoff between state size and completeness. It says what happens to events past the watermark, such as dropping them or routing them to a late-data table for a correction job. It also proposes a reconciliation step that compares streaming totals with a batch recount for a sample window.\",\"targets\":[\"Streaming\"],\"tests\":[]},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"prompt\":\"Implement solve(input) in JavaScript. The input is a string of lines, each line of the form \\\"timestamp,key\\\" where timestamp is a non-negative integer number of seconds. Group events into tumbling 60-second windows, where a window starts at floor(timestamp/60)*60. Count events per (window start, key). Return lines of the form \\\"windowStart,key,count\\\", sorted by windowStart ascending and then by key ascending, joined with \\\\n and no trailing newline.\",\"rubric\":\"A correct solution parses each line, computes the window start with integer math, and counts with a map keyed on the pair. It sorts numerically by window start and then lexically by key before joining. Watch for sorting window starts as strings and for a trailing newline in the output.\",\"targets\":[\"Streaming\"],\"tests\":[{\"input\":\"0,a\\n30,a\\n61,a\\n62,b\",\"expected\":\"0,a,2\\n60,a,1\\n60,b,1\"},{\"input\":\"5,x\\n5,x\\n5,x\",\"expected\":\"0,x,3\"},{\"input\":\"119,k\\n120,k\",\"expected\":\"60,k,1\\n120,k,1\"}]}],\"emailSubject\":\"Data Engineer (mid) assessment\",\"emailBody\":\"Hi,\\n\\nThank you for applying for the Data Engineer (mid) role. We would like you to complete a short assessment before we go further.\\n\\nThere are three tasks. One is a short written question. One is a scenario about a streaming pipeline. The last is a small coding exercise in JavaScript. The coding task is a single function called solve.\\n\\nPlease allow about 45 minutes in total. Work on your own and answer in your own words.\\n\\nReply to this email with your answers when you are done. If anything in the instructions is unclear, ask us before you start.\\n\\nThe hiring team\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":522,"output":1674,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0008892,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426619989},"b161b8a5f18a798193e7a3243c43e81a551d6e751d71237a860fb6fa2ee83122":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01PLPDpZmABtRMknQwW9BTSK","name":"send_assessment","input":{"reason":"Assessments created for all five approved candidates targeting their unverified gaps; sending now.","candidate_ids":["c030","c041","c171","c051","c118"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":27609,"output":123,"cacheRead":2979,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00285219,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426621288},"181435e9a059c732948e92ce39db9cce606add54ee7d0a507a31fa7ba8416f3c":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_019e2JPWZHXB5wZE6qeY5oW5","name":"collect_responses","input":{"reason":"Assessments were sent to all five approved candidates; collecting their answers so they can be graded.","candidate_ids":["c030","c041","c171","c051","c118"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":29074,"output":124,"cacheRead":2979,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00299919,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426622451},"95a5821fb300206e42be21e08be245504220ed4da8dd735aab22d1084f328d34":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"answer\":\"Retries control how many times a single task instance is re-run after it fails, with a delay between attempts (retries and retry_delay). They only apply within one run of one task. catchup is a DAG-level setting that controls whether the scheduler creates and runs the missed scheduled intervals between the DAG's start_date and now when it is first enabled or unpaused. With catchup=True, enabling a DAG with a start_date months back produces a large backfill. I would set catchup=False for a DAG that reads 'current state' rather than per-interval data, such as a job that refreshes a snapshot table from a live API. Running hundreds of stale intervals there would waste compute and overwrite newer results with older ones.\"},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"answer\":\"I would first make each partition load idempotent so any rerun is safe. For each of the 200 partitions I would write with a delete-then-insert or MERGE keyed on the partition date, inside a transaction where the warehouse supports it, or write to a staging partition and swap it in atomically. Re-running partition 37 then replaces its rows instead of appending to them. Second, I would split the single task into one mapped task per partition, or at least use a loop with per-partition checkpoints, so a failure at partition 121 does not force a re-run of partitions 1 to 120. Third, I would make the DAG's rerun behaviour explicit: retries on the task with a sensible delay, a clear rule for which partitions a 'clear and rerun' will touch, and a row-count check after each partition that compares source and target counts. Finally, I would add a post-run validation task that asserts all 200 partitions exist with expected counts and fails the DAG loudly if any are missing or duplicated. This turns silent duplication or gaps into a visible failure at the end of the run.\"},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"answer\":\"function solve(input) {\\n  const s = (input || \\\"\\\").trim();\\n  if (s === \\\"\\\") return \\\"\\\";\\n  const nodes = new Set();\\n  const adj = new Map();\\n  const indeg = new Map();\\n  for (const raw of s.split(\\\",\\\")) {\\n    const e = raw.trim();\\n    if (!e) continue;\\n    const [a, b] = e.split(\\\">\\\").map(x => x.trim());\\n    nodes.add(a);\\n    nodes.add(b);\\n    if (!adj.has(a)) adj.set(a, new Set());\\n    if (!adj.has(b)) adj.set(b, new Set());\\n    if (!adj.get(a).has(b)) {\\n      adj.get(a).add(b);\\n      indeg.set(b, (indeg.get(b) || 0) + 1);\\n    }\\n  }\\n  for (const n of nodes) {\\n    if (!indeg.has(n)) indeg.set(n, 0);\\n  }\\n  const ready = [...nodes].filter(n => indeg.get(n) === 0);\\n  const order = [];\\n  while (ready.length > 0) {\\n    ready.sort();\\n    const n = ready.shift();\\n    order.push(n);\\n    for (const m of adj.get(n)) {\\n      const d = indeg.get(m) - 1;\\n      indeg.set(m, d);\\n      if (d === 0) ready.push(m);\\n    }\\n  }\\n  if (order.length !== nodes.size) return \\\"cycle\\\";\\n  return order.join(\\\" \\\");\\n}\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":835,"output":1975,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.001071,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426630999},"65a3e8912f3a21e9f954720d0a297478593475b9433d01d66be73aec8fa5b556":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"answer\":\"WHERE filters rows before they are grouped, and HAVING filters groups after aggregation. You would need HAVING when you want to keep only groups that meet a condition on an aggregate, like customers with more than 5 orders.\"},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"answer\":\"I would compare the report totals with the source system and look for where the numbers diverge. Likely causes are a join that duplicates rows, so I'd check for duplicate keys in the customers table. To fix it, I'd make sure the join keys are unique and add a test on the reporting table so it gets flagged next time.\"},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"answer\":\"function solve(input) {\\n  const totals = {};\\n  const lines = input.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\");\\n  for (const line of lines) {\\n    const [user, amount] = line.split(\\\",\\\");\\n    totals[user] = (totals[user] || 0) + parseInt(amount);\\n  }\\n  return Object.keys(totals).sort().map(u => u + \\\",\\\" + totals[u]).join(\\\"\\\\n\\\");\\n}\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":765,"output":893,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.000523,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426635684},"13a052d081da2e1ec4cf1f14fee1142eaf6cecc13943113d0ee59571feb4094b":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"answer\":\"At-least-once delivery means every event is delivered one or more times, so retries after a failure can produce duplicates. Exactly-once means each event's effect is applied one time. In practice, exactly-once is usually achieved as at-least-once delivery plus idempotent or transactional writes at the sink. One concrete way to make a sink safe against replay is to give every event a deterministic key, such as a hash of source topic, partition, and offset, or a business key from the producer. Then write with an upsert or MERGE keyed on that value, so reprocessing the same event overwrites the same row instead of inserting a second one.\"},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"answer\":\"I would use event time rather than processing time. I would parse the event timestamp from the payload and set withWatermark on it to about 2 hours plus a small margin, so late mobile events still fall inside the allowed lateness. Then I would group by window(eventTime, '1 hour') and page, and count. Because the watermark bounds state, Spark can drop old window state once the watermark passes it. I would write in update or append output mode. Append mode emits each window only after it is final, which is cleaner for a warehouse table. For a table that must stay correct, I would MERGE each emitted window into the target on (window_start, page) so late corrections replace earlier values rather than duplicate them. Events that arrive after the watermark are dropped by Spark, so I would not rely on silent loss. I would route them to a side path by comparing event time to the current watermark, or keep the raw Kafka topic and run a periodic batch backfill over a longer horizon to correct any windows that closed too early. To validate, I would run a daily batch recount over the raw events for the same hours, using the same window definition, and compare per-page, per-hour counts to the streaming table. Any gap above a small tolerance would trigger an alert, and I would check whether the mismatch is explained by events past the watermark. I would track the late-drop count as a metric so the 2-hour assumption can be revisited with data.\"},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"answer\":\"function solve(input) {\\n  const counts = new Map();\\n  for (const raw of input.split('\\\\n')) {\\n    const line = raw.replace(/\\\\r$/, '');\\n    if (line.trim() === '') continue;\\n    const idx = line.indexOf(',');\\n    if (idx < 0) continue;\\n    const ts = parseInt(line.slice(0, idx), 10);\\n    const key = line.slice(idx + 1);\\n    const start = Math.floor(ts / 60) * 60;\\n    const id = JSON.stringify([start, key]);\\n    const entry = counts.get(id);\\n    if (entry) {\\n      entry.count += 1;\\n    } else {\\n      counts.set(id, { start, key, count: 1 });\\n    }\\n  }\\n  const rows = Array.from(counts.values());\\n  rows.sort((a, b) => {\\n    if (a.start !== b.start) return a.start - b.start;\\n    if (a.key < b.key) return -1;\\n    if (a.key > b.key) return 1;\\n    return 0;\\n  });\\n  return rows.map(r => `${r.start},${r.key},${r.count}`).join('\\\\n');\\n}\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":811,"output":1648,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0009051,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426643836},"0091b550756e99d7f452549395b0a2615eb0ba820e06e28e16848d04b073cf40":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"answer\":\"At-least-once delivery guarantees that every record is processed one or more times, so nothing is lost but duplicates can occur, typically after a retry that follows a failure before the offset or acknowledgement was committed. Exactly-once means each record's effect is applied one time. In practice, exactly-once is achieved end to end by combining replayable sources, checkpointed state, and idempotent or transactional sinks, not by the transport alone. A concrete way to make a sink safe against replays is to write with a deterministic natural key and use an upsert or MERGE, for example MERGE INTO the target on (page_id, window_start) so a replayed batch overwrites the same row rather than inserting a second one. Alternatively, store the Kafka partition and offset range with each batch and skip batches already recorded in a control table, all within the same transaction as the write.\"},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"answer\":\"I would parse the Kafka value into a typed schema and use the event-time timestamp, not the processing time, for windowing. The aggregation would be groupBy(window(event_time, '1 hour'), page_id).count(). For lateness, I would set withWatermark('event_time', '2 hours') so that Spark keeps state for each hourly window until the watermark passes its end plus the allowed lateness, which is enough to cover the two-hour mobile delay. I would run the query in append output mode, so each window is emitted once, only after the watermark closes it. That means the final hourly counts appear roughly two to three hours after the hour ends, which is a tradeoff the downstream consumers should be told about.\\n\\nFor events arriving after the watermark, Spark drops them from the stateful aggregation. I would not silently accept that. I would route them to a side path: use a foreachBatch or a separate query that filters on event_time below the current watermark and writes them to a quarantine table with ingest time, so the drop rate is measured and alerted on. If the drop rate is material, I would raise the watermark threshold or handle corrections with an update-mode table that upserts revised counts.\\n\\nTo validate, I would run a nightly batch job over the raw Kafka archive or landing files for the same hours, compute the same window counts with a plain groupBy, and full-outer-join them against the streaming output on (window_start, page_id). Any mismatch beyond the quarantine count is a bug to investigate. I would track row-level reconciliation totals per day as a dbt test or a Databricks data quality check so regressions surface quickly.\"},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"answer\":\"function solve(input) {\\n  const counts = new Map();\\n  for (const raw of input.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\")) {\\n    const line = raw.trim();\\n    if (line === \\\"\\\") continue;\\n    const idx = line.indexOf(\\\",\\\");\\n    if (idx < 0) continue;\\n    const ts = Number(line.slice(0, idx));\\n    const key = line.slice(idx + 1);\\n    const start = Math.floor(ts / 60) * 60;\\n    const mapKey = JSON.stringify([start, key]);\\n    const entry = counts.get(mapKey);\\n    if (entry) {\\n      entry.count += 1;\\n    } else {\\n      counts.set(mapKey, { start, key, count: 1 });\\n    }\\n  }\\n  const entries = Array.from(counts.values());\\n  entries.sort((a, b) => {\\n    if (a.start !== b.start) return a.start - b.start;\\n    if (a.key < b.key) return -1;\\n    if (a.key > b.key) return 1;\\n    return 0;\\n  });\\n  return entries.map(e => `${e.start},${e.key},${e.count}`).join(\\\"\\\\n\\\");\\n}\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":822,"output":1883,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0010237,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426653234},"c130c2b2d81082cd92187ef6ed4f96b3ee095a997c4475aef8f08780ccf9cce5":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"answer\":\"Retries are how many times Airflow re-runs a single task instance after it fails, with a delay between attempts. Catchup controls whether the scheduler creates DAG runs for past schedule intervals that were missed since start_date. Set catchup to False for a daily DAG that refreshes a current snapshot, such as pulling today's exchange rates, where backfilling every missed day would just repeat work and load stale data.\"},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"answer\":\"I would make each partition load idempotent first. Each partition gets written with a delete-then-insert or MERGE keyed on the partition date, so rerunning it replaces the rows rather than appending them. Then I would rerun only the failed or incomplete partitions instead of the whole 200, using a control table that records status per partition with a run ID. In the DAG, I would split the load into one mapped task per partition with a small max_active_tasks limit so a failure is contained and retries are per partition. I would also add a row-count check after each partition against the source, and a final reconciliation task that fails the run if any partition is missing or duplicated. Alerts would go out when a partition fails more than its retry budget.\"},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"answer\":\"function solve(input) {\\n  if (!input || input.trim() === '') return '';\\n  const edges = input.split(',').map(e => e.trim().split('>'));\\n  const indeg = {};\\n  const adj = {};\\n  for (const [a, b] of edges) {\\n    if (!(a in indeg)) indeg[a] = 0;\\n    if (!(b in indeg)) indeg[b] = 0;\\n    if (!adj[a]) adj[a] = [];\\n    adj[a].push(b);\\n    indeg[b]++;\\n  }\\n  const ready = Object.keys(indeg).filter(n => indeg[n] === 0);\\n  const order = [];\\n  while (ready.length) {\\n    const n = ready.shift();\\n    order.push(n);\\n    for (const m of (adj[n] || [])) {\\n      indeg[m]--;\\n      if (indeg[m] === 0) ready.push(m);\\n    }\\n  }\\n  if (order.length !== Object.keys(indeg).length) return 'cycle';\\n  return order.join(' ');\\n}\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":838,"output":1414,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0007907999999999999,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426660543},"11cfbcc31deee2697d424c06b786344a429640b0c0ae09bc8e05ada9355affcf":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01A8pMYXzJFeZnaveywFMvbA","name":"run_code","input":{"reason":"Responses collected for all five; each candidate submitted a JavaScript solve() function, so run them against the hidden tests.","candidate_ids":["c030","c041","c171","c051","c118"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":31218,"output":131,"cacheRead":2979,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00321709,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426661975},"b76a482b8831b14c32bf8d87471da7e62f94c9101f79f1996724e22ce228afcb":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_011hzHXygATPLoSqvN1WExow","name":"grade_answer","input":{"reason":"Sandbox results are in (c030 3/3, c041 1/3, c171 3/3, c051 3/3, c118 2/3); grade the rubric answers, cross-checking against the test results.","candidate_ids":["c030","c041","c171","c051","c118"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":32424,"output":158,"cacheRead":2979,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00335119,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426663209},"3c846162eb871b41d85d5089319020ee9d1e67f28ee657853a9321445b49034a":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"yes\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"rationale\":\"Correctly distinguishes task retries (re-running a failed task instance within one DAG run, with retry_delay) from DAG-level catchup (creating runs for missed intervals since start_date when enabled). Gives a concrete, sound example of catchup=False: a snapshot refresh from a live API where replaying stale intervals wastes compute and overwrites newer data. Meets the expected qualities.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":553,"output":153,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0001318,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426664743},"cdd74b112639373c4a37c45a8342b94ae1bc5f0ac50172adecafbcfb1b3dafb7":{"response":{"text":"{\"parts\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"score\":0.9,\"confidence\":0.85,\"note\":\"Correctly distinguishes retries (per task instance within a run) from catchup (scheduler creating missed intervals), and gives a concrete snapshot-refresh example for catchup=False.\"},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"score\":0.85,\"confidence\":0.8,\"note\":\"Covers idempotent per-partition writes via delete-then-insert or MERGE, per-partition checkpointing, retries, row-count verification and a post-run validation task; it does not explicitly mention logical date or data interval, which the rubric asks for.\"},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"score\":1.0,\"confidence\":0.95,\"note\":\"Uses in-degree map with Kahn's algorithm, a sorted ready list picking the smallest node, and cycle detection by comparing emitted count to node count; hidden tests passed 3/3.\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":1906,"output":300,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00034060000000000004,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426666576},"023b6c4804ecf3ba303ecc09ad6502c76a0092bc5dcf6f15e1bdaa55187722f4":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"yes\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"rationale\":\"The answer correctly states that WHERE filters rows before grouping and HAVING filters groups after aggregation. Its example, keeping customers with more than 5 orders, uses an aggregate condition that WHERE cannot express. It is brief and lacks a concrete SQL snippet, but it meets the expected qualities.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":362,"output":127,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0000997,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426667566},"07e1ed5a6ce5af269c6ae05bee0be43e3c8fe867d7b28a39c2fc89c39edb8759":{"response":{"text":"{\"parts\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"score\":0.9,\"confidence\":0.85,\"note\":\"Correctly distinguishes WHERE (row filter before grouping) from HAVING (group filter after aggregation) and gives a valid COUNT-based example, though briefly.\"},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"score\":0.6,\"confidence\":0.7,\"note\":\"Identifies duplicate rows from a non-unique join key and proposes enforcing uniqueness plus a reconciliation test, but omits explicit row-count checks before and after the join.\"},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"score\":0.15,\"confidence\":0.9,\"note\":\"Does not skip blank lines, so empty input or a trailing newline produces a bogus user entry with NaN; hidden tests passed only 1 of 3, consistent with this failure.\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":1023,"output":545,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0003748,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426670483},"f1bb683f8baab2f2a2256ceed27b183072a1f235794503e10f822b1a1e434a65":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"yes\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"rationale\":\"Correctly distinguishes at-least-once (possible duplicates on retry) from exactly-once (effect applied once), notes exactly-once is achieved via at-least-once plus idempotent or transactional sink writes, and gives a concrete fix: a deterministic key (source topic/partition/offset hash or business key) with upsert/MERGE so replays overwrite the same row. Minor gap: it does not mention checkpointing or offset-tied transactions, but the core expectations are met.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":538,"output":182,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00014480000000000002,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426671998},"a4c30f2897625dbc382f435791a475db72eda31a8b938a7cb1e1faf04fe6a3be":{"response":{"text":"{\"parts\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"score\":0.9,\"confidence\":0.85,\"note\":\"Explains at-least-once duplicates versus end-to-end exactly-once via idempotent or transactional sinks, with a concrete deterministic-key MERGE fix; checkpointing is only implied.\"},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"score\":0.8,\"confidence\":0.8,\"note\":\"Covers event-time windows, a 2-hour-plus watermark, late-event handling via side path or backfill, and batch reconciliation; the state-size versus completeness tradeoff is only touched on.\"},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"score\":1.0,\"confidence\":0.95,\"note\":\"Correct integer window math, pair-keyed counting, numeric window sort, lexical key sort, and no trailing newline; hidden tests pass 3/3.\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":1875,"output":680,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0005275,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426675475},"c894d601ff4b12d0ec34b77f1bd13ebc17e58bd917254198b7e2be0df43aa1ee":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"yes\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"rationale\":\"Correctly explains at-least-once as guaranteeing no loss with possible duplicates after retries before offset commit, and exactly-once as an end-to-end property requiring replayable sources, checkpointed state, and idempotent or transactional sinks. Gives two concrete, specific fixes: MERGE on a deterministic natural key (page_id, window_start) and offset-tracking in a control table within the same transaction. Minor gap: it does not explicitly say the source must be replayable for the MERGE approach to matter, but it is acceptable overall.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":613,"output":202,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00016230000000000001,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426676855},"e489e63cef24aea6a993ab1d490c509f2db8dcafa275e2570da39bb7d943f751":{"response":{"text":"{\"parts\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"score\":0.9,\"confidence\":0.85,\"note\":\"Clearly distinguishes at-least-once from exactly-once as an end-to-end property and gives two concrete fixes (MERGE on a natural key, offset-tracked control table in the same transaction).\"},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"score\":0.85,\"confidence\":0.8,\"note\":\"Covers event-time windows, a 2-hour watermark with the state tradeoff noted, a quarantine path for late events, and a batch reconciliation; the update-mode correction remark is slightly muddled.\"},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"score\":1.0,\"confidence\":0.95,\"note\":\"Hidden tests pass 3/3; uses integer window math, a composite-key map, numeric sort on window start, lexical key sort, and no trailing newline.\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":2018,"output":480,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0004418,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426679740},"3c8fa24f20e84e0aa0c4015ee766e6c1151eb1bcf2bbc518c1a570cc00fdcb23":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"yes\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"rationale\":\"Correctly distinguishes retries (re-running a failed task instance with a delay) from catchup (creating runs for missed past schedule intervals since start_date). The example of a daily snapshot DAG pulling current exchange rates, where backfill would repeat work and load stale data, matches the expected concrete case. Minor gap: it does not explicitly say retries stay within the same DAG run, but the core distinction is clear.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":463,"output":159,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00012580000000000002,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793426681131},"dabfbf36112e93f8bc8343c64bdb874968fe18f5b7d7bc834ed59da7677565e6":{"response":{"text":"{\"parts\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"score\":0.95,\"confidence\":0.9,\"note\":\"Correctly separates retries (re-running a task instance within a DAG run) from catchup (creating runs for missed past intervals) and gives a concrete catchup=False case.\"},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"score\":0.75,\"confidence\":0.8,\"note\":\"Covers idempotent delete-then-insert/MERGE, per-partition retries, a control table, and row-count reconciliation, but never mentions using the logical date or data interval instead of wall-clock time.\"},{\"kind\":\"code\",