","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01AU7wUGn2odySdRcqMH3xw8","name":"get_profile","input":{"reason":"c118 is a data-engineering profile with PySpark, DAG orchestration and Kafka; verify must-haves for a fifth shortlist slot.","candidate_id":"c118"}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":20528,"output":257,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0022135,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793428089346},"a1132adb9bcb50f14fe75e7caddc79a6f52c324eebaff9cdb429763e93eb7a89":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01Ph4jbS7QK8CreQNc22XQVY","name":"cite_evidence","input":{"reason":"Pranav Saxena's profile shows PySpark batch rebuilds, dbt/Snowflake SQL modelling with tests, and Kafka streaming consumers; cite for m1, m2, m3, n2.","candidate_id":"c118","citations":[{"requirement_id":"m1","quote":"Rebuilt nightly PySpark jobs on Databricks and cut the batch window from 6 hours to 50 minutes."},{"requirement_id":"m2","quote":"Wrote the window-function queries and tested dbt models that reconcile the finance and product marts."},{"requirement_id":"m3","quote":"Modelled a Snowflake warehouse with dbt, with tests on every mart."},{"requirement_id":"n2","quote":"Wrote Kafka consumers that land clickstream events in BigQuery within a minute."}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":22569,"output":373,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0024756,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793428091157},"226a4cc9f032b23cd95557e1e7f44d788b83ce449d41275c08a5fa58546bd827":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"yes\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"rationale\":\"The sentence describes concrete hands-on work: modelling a Snowflake warehouse with dbt and adding tests to every mart. That is built data-pipeline work, not a keyword list or aspirational statement.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":349,"output":92,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0000809,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793428092753},"67ae9eb619cbb4678bec89cc7fb35cb6dade1f229b458d6f937f811df30aebe0":{"response":{"text":"Five candidates are now fully evidenced on all three must-haves, which meets the target. I'll request HR approval for this shortlist.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01CVpNiNeU7gYi5ZWX7B8L6A","name":"request_hr_approval","input":{"reason":"Five candidates each have verbatim evidence for all three must-haves (Spark, SQL, data pipelines), so the target shortlist of 5 is met.","candidate_ids":["c030","c051","c041","c171","c118"],"rationale":"Shortlist of 5 mid-level Data Engineer candidates, each with cited evidence for Spark, SQL and data pipelines. Nice-to-haves: Airflow is evidenced for c051 and c171; streaming (Kafka consumers) is evidenced for c030, c041, c118 and c171. Reformulations used: 0 of 2. Tool calls used: 13 of 25."}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":24369,"output":342,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0026401,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793428095014},"b04eebdf52a7613c21c2420370b0ec2335ca2821e8430b3653433a5420084329":{"response":{"text":"Every approved candidate is fully evidenced on the must-haves, so the assessment should target the nice-to-haves each profile leaves unverified. Airflow is not evidenced for c030, c041 or c118, and streaming is not evidenced for c051.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01S8iTh1Bo9GFcYPufftxctQ","name":"create_assessment","input":{"reason":"All five approved candidates cover the must-haves, so questions should probe the nice-to-haves their profiles don't evidence: Airflow for c030, c041, c118 and streaming for c051; c171's streaming evidence is only partial.","candidates":[{"candidate_id":"c030","unverified_requirement_ids":["n1"]},{"candidate_id":"c051","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]},{"candidate_id":"c041","unverified_requirement_ids":["n1"]},{"candidate_id":"c171","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]},{"candidate_id":"c118","unverified_requirement_ids":["n1"]}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":25932,"output":536,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0028918500000000005,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793428099095},"5415006143bc0299274ff6598aa428bed9ecd98ac0886a10f5686b0422e51d4f":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01A7ALr6cHCw3b5LA8SRn53t","name":"send_assessment","input":{"reason":"All five approved candidates have assessments generated, each targeting only the nice-to-have their profile does not evidence, so the next step is to send them.","candidate_ids":["c030","c051","c041","c171","c118"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":28886,"output":139,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0029887500000000005,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793428100394},"9acdb1cfd3ee7b83c3e685554bbce19c9cc80bf38c44ec797e04e44444890093":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01XwehU3ferK1SwoYUEhxyeM","name":"collect_responses","input":{"reason":"Assessments were sent to all five approved candidates; collect their answers so they can be run and graded.","candidate_ids":["c030","c051","c041","c171","c118"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":30546,"output":125,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0031477500000000004,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793428101558},"ba55431930d977b2d510baea4cbacae83d314da1aeef0f9b45735f8372a2daad":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"answer\":\"At-most-once can lose records, at-least-once never loses them but can redeliver duplicates after a retry or replay, and exactly-once means the observable result is as if each record was processed one time. True end-to-end exactly-once needs cooperation from the sink. In practice I get it by making the sink idempotent. For example, I write each batch with a MERGE keyed on a stable event_id (or on the Kafka topic, partition, and offset), so replaying the same records updates existing rows instead of inserting new ones. Spark's foreachBatch with a deterministic batch id, or a transactional sink that commits offsets atomically with the data, works the same way.\"},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"answer\":\"I would use event time, not processing time. I would parse the event timestamp from the payload, then set withWatermark(\\\"event_time\\\", \\\"2 hours\\\") so state for each window is retained long enough to absorb the late mobile events. Then I would aggregate with groupBy(window(\\\"event_time\\\", \\\"1 hour\\\"), \\\"page\\\").count() and use the update or append output mode. Append mode is the right choice for a warehouse table, because each window is emitted once, after the watermark passes its end. The trade-off is that results arrive up to about three hours after the window closes. Events older than the watermark are dropped by Spark, so I would not rely on silent drops. I would route them to a side stream or dead-letter topic, with a counter metric, by comparing event_time against the current watermark in a separate query or in a foreachBatch step. Because late data can still arrive after a window is finalized, I would write to the warehouse using an idempotent MERGE keyed on (window_start, page). Then any correction is an upsert, not a duplicate. To verify, I would run a nightly batch recount of the raw Kafka landing data for the same hours, using the same window logic, and compare it to the streaming table. I would alert on any window where the difference exceeds a small tolerance, and check the dropped-event counter to explain any gaps.\"},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"answer\":\"function solve(input) {\\n  const byStart = new Map();\\n  for (const raw of input.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\")) {\\n    const line = raw.replace(/\\\\r$/, \\\"\\\");\\n    if (line.trim() === \\\"\\\") continue;\\n    const idx = line.indexOf(\\\",\\\");\\n    const ts = Number(line.slice(0, idx).trim());\\n    const key = line.slice(idx + 1);\\n    const start = Math.floor(ts / 60) * 60;\\n    if (!byStart.has(start)) byStart.set(start, new Map());\\n    const inner = byStart.get(start);\\n    inner.set(key, (inner.get(key) || 0) + 1);\\n  }\\n  const rows = [];\\n  for (const [start, inner] of byStart) {\\n    for (const [key, count] of inner) {\\n      rows.push({ start, key, count });\\n    }\\n  }\\n  rows.sort((a, b) => {\\n    if (a.start !== b.start) return a.start - b.start;\\n    if (a.key < b.key) return -1;\\n    if (a.key > b.key) return 1;\\n    return 0;\\n  });\\n  return rows.map(r => `${r.start},${r.key},${r.count}`).join(\\\"\\\\n\\\");\\n}\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":809,"output":1973,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0010674,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793428110405},"1e8f56a4140304fd4735da369f6a9c94c672da2acdbf485c6b6ca5b563f00447":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01HqoLcgZ1r6BfzrD51DAxKn","name":"run_code","input":{"reason":"Responses collected for all five; run each candidate's JavaScript answer against the hidden tests to get objective coding results.","candidate_ids":["c030","c051","c041","c171","c118"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":32955,"output":130,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00339115,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793428111615},"bd19f778a5b0ebda4e28a83c50ccc9e3e36e0fcea767410849aa4c0b4578bc3d":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01YWCpHYxRBVC9Gy5WGBoWBD","name":"grade_answer","input":{"reason":"Sandbox results are in (c030 3/3, c051 3/3, c171 3/3, c118 2/3, c041 1/3); grade the rubric answers for all five so the scores can be compared against the tests.","candidate_ids":["c030","c051","c041","c171","c118"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":34376,"output":163,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0035497500000000004,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793428112904},"d9bf71f717dfbca76818238a1ad4bad182161a8192425400859825eb56eba1fe":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"yes\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"rationale\":\"Correctly distinguishes at-most-once, at-least-once (duplicates on replay), and exactly-once as an observable/end-to-end property requiring sink cooperation. Gives a concrete, correct fix: MERGE keyed on event_id or topic/partition/offset so replays are idempotent, plus transactional offset-data commits as an alternative. Covers the expected qualities without significant errors.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":552,"output":155,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0001327,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793428114338},"23eadebea932cc8f8a6b7593d41b28f6e3b4a8b8ee0ef0293dce264b3db3ef04":{"response":{"text":"{\"parts\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"score\":0.9,\"confidence\":0.85,\"note\":\"Correctly distinguishes at-least-once duplicates from end-to-end exactly-once and gives concrete idempotent fixes (MERGE on event_id or topic/partition/offset, foreachBatch, transactional offset commits).\"},{\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"score\":0.85,\"confidence\":0.8,\"note\":\"Covers event-time windows, a 2-hour watermark with its latency tradeoff, dropping and routing late events to a side path, idempotent MERGE for corrections, and batch-recount reconciliation; state-size tradeoff is only lightly addressed.\"},{\"kind\":\"code\",\"score\":1.0,\"confidence\":0.9,\"note\":\"Hidden tests pass 3/3; uses integer window math, a nested map for counts, numeric window sort, lexical key sort, and no trailing newline.\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":1880,"output":750,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.000563,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793428118246},"2b80486fed89d61c33499e1bfa72dc7b427e556b95d89c4235180ea3deb99bde":{"response":{"text":"All five approved candidates now have graded assessments. The rubric scores agree with the sandbox results (c041 scored 1/3 on tests and 0.15 on code; c118 scored 2/3 and 0.30), and n