pipeline depth for c066 and c122 (only one implied sentence).","candidates":[{"candidate_id":"c049","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]},{"candidate_id":"c156","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]},{"candidate_id":"c002","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]},{"candidate_id":"c066","unverified_requirement_ids":["n1"]},{"candidate_id":"c122","unverified_requirement_ids":["n1"]}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":32617,"output":1082,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0038333500000000006,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429015177},"faf7fc5c1679df31d54f9bd5d1c6a74cd7944962f9d31f63de872d1215734f5e":{"response":{"text":"Four candidates are now fully evidenced (c154, c006, c108, c047). I'll run one more search for a fifth before requesting approval.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01C3v3EG6geuybpSjCJfnyCc","name":"search_candidates","input":{"reason":"Four candidates are fully evidenced and the target is five, so I'm running one more Next.js/Preact plus screen-reader query to look for a fifth.","query":"practitioner building Next.js and Preact UIs with screen-reader testing and strict typing","mode":"semantic","k":12}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":33730,"output":314,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0035621999999999997,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429015958},"ba820af5c6c0ce44123a1a8b45f48d255a64c11ab04c2253df755b90602bb7eb":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01GeJ7ZurKpNRzd1JqA4DamR","name":"get_profile","input":{"candidate_id":"c079","reason":"c079 (Noor Saxena) appeared with WCAG, strict-typing and LCP/CLS in the snippet and was approved in earlier sessions; checking her profile for the fifth slot."}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":36968,"output":184,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.003821,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429017682},"d84927e92df84285fcb782531eaa1c2b5c6fb56213f6eaf301c487aca5507363":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_014VPH42TP1Vv7iX9bJjfi13","name":"cite_evidence","input":{"candidate_id":"c079","reason":"c079's profile has a Next.js/Preact sentence (accepted partial React proxy), strict-typing and WCAG sentences, and Storybook/LCP sentences for the nice-to-haves; citing them in one call.","citations":[{"requirement_id":"m1","quote":"Practitioner working on storybook-driven components."},{"requirement_id":"m2","quote":"Migrated a JavaScript codebase to strict typing with zero any escapes."},{"requirement_id":"m3","quote":"Rebuilt the component kit under strict static typing (tsc --strict, zero any) with ARIA roles, keyboard navigation and screen-reader labels on every control."},{"requirement_id":"n1","quote":"Shipped a shared UI kit with theme tokens adopted by six product squads, with Storybook docs for every piece."},{"requirement_id":"n2","quote":"Brought Largest Contentful Paint under 1.5s on the marketing site by code-splitting and image work."}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":39553,"output":434,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0042045,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429019931},"c2a61fc724f64baa799521a87a274dbb1e046fcfffa4d644cd5a9ccb210ca4d5":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"no\",\"confidence\":0.55,\"rationale\":\"The sentence describes concrete work on components via Storybook, but it never names React. Storybook supports several UI frameworks (Vue, Angular, React), so it does not reliably imply React the way FastAPI implies Python. The evidence is suggestive but not sufficient.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":332,"output":299,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0001827,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429021954},"8b4e40c7e6829dc6c0a228c7a2498b03b126722ae7e88976a2722c5806433da1":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"q-001\",\"type\":\"mcq\",\"prompt\":\"In a FastAPI service with separate ingestion and query endpoints, which approach best keeps request validation separate from business logic?\",\"skills\":[\"FastAPI\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"answerKey\":\"Declare Pydantic models as endpoint parameter and response types, and call a service-layer function from the route handler\",\"options\":[\"Declare Pydantic models as endpoint parameter and response types, and call a service-layer function from the route handler\",\"Parse and validate raw request bodies manually inside each route handler before running the query logic\",\"Store validation rules in a global dictionary that the query functions read at runtime\",\"Disable automatic validation so that each service function controls its own input checks\"],\"estimatedMinutes\":8,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-003\"]},{\"id\":\"q-002\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"You need an asyncio worker that consumes jobs from an asyncio.Queue, processes them concurrently with a limit of 10 in flight, and shuts down cleanly when cancelled. Describe the design and the key code-level decisions you would make, including how you handle exceptions in individual jobs and how you ensure in-flight work is not silently lost on shutdown.\",\"skills\":[\"asyncio\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"rubric\":\"Score 0-4. Full credit (4) requires: bounded concurrency via asyncio.Semaphore or a fixed set of worker tasks; a worker loop that awaits queue.get() and calls queue.task_done() in a finally block; per-job exception handling so one failure does not crash the worker; handling of asyncio.CancelledError with re-raise, plus draining or cancelling tasks via asyncio.gather or TaskGroup with an explicit shutdown sequence. Deduct 1 for each missing element, 1 more if the answer blocks the event loop (e.g. synchronous I/O or CPU work without run_in_executor). Score 0 if the answer does not use asyncio concepts.\",\"estimatedMinutes\":12,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-004\"]},{\"id\":\"q-003\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"A team owns a public REST API that several internal clients depend on. Describe how you would manage the API contract over time: how you define it, how you detect breaking changes before release, and how you communicate and version a change that must break existing clients.\",\"skills\":[\"REST API contract ownership\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"rubric\":\"Score 0-4. Full credit (4) requires: a source of truth for the contract (e.g. OpenAPI spec or schema-first definition) reviewed alongside code; an automated compatibility check in CI (e.g. diffing the spec against the previous release to flag removed fields, changed types, or newly required parameters); a versioning strategy for breaking changes (URL or header versioning, or a deprecation window with sunset dates); and client communication (changelog, advance notice, migration guide). Deduct 1 for each missing element. Score 0 if the answer does not address contracts or compatibility.\",\"estimatedMinutes\":12,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-009\"]}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":803,"output":1376,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0007683,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429023091},"1da9847af6fc8a055b4e5586e8e61364f09eb00772673239aaee3d9d1e5d6b86":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_015X3kCGkj7FcXaxeNZNjrfW","name":"cite_evidence","input":{"candidate_id":"c079","reason":"c079's React citation was rejected for lacking an explicit React mention; per memory, the Next.js/Preact UI line is the accepted partial React proxy, so I'm retrying once with it.","citations":[{"requirement_id":"m1","quote":"Next.js and Preact UIs"}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":42113,"output":336,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0044115,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429023970},"3c64dbc7eaef98b707283451f9d5caa1b949bd4ca074d7a145d40734ff2882e8":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"id\":\"q1\",\"answer\":\"I would start by confirming the regression is real and scoped. I'd rerun the failing prompt against the old and new model versions with pinned sampling settings, using the held-out set plus the specific cases that broke, and compare pass rates per rubric dimension. That separates a broad regression from a few edge cases, and it tells me whether the failure is in format, content, refusals, or tool-call behavior. I'd also diff the release notes and any known behavior changes for the new version, and check whether my own inputs, retrieval results, or system prompt changed in the same window.\\n\\nOnce I know which dimension broke, I'd find the cause by isolating it. Typical causes are a shift in instruction following (the model now ignores a constraint it used to follow), a change in output formatting, or a change in how it handles a borderline refusal. I'd inspect a handful of failing traces side by side with passing ones and look for the pattern rather than patching individual examples.\\n\\nThe fix depends on the cause. Often I'd tighten the prompt, make the output contract more explicit, or add a few targeted examples, then rerun the full graded set so I don't trade one failure for another. If the new version is worse overall, I'd pin the old version, record the regression in the release log with the eval results, and gate the upgrade on the eval suite passing so this is caught before rollout next time.\"},{\"id\":\"q2\",\"answer\":\"I'd design the prompt around a strict output contract. The model returns only a JSON object with exactly four keys: vendor (string or null), date (ISO 8601 YYYY-MM-DD or null), total (number, using a dot decimal and no currency symbols or thousands separators, or null), and currency (ISO 4217 three-letter code or null). I'd state the rules for each field, including what to do when a value is missing or ambiguous: return null rather than guess. For example, if the text shows two dates, I'd specify which one counts as the invoice date and tell the model to null the field if it can't tell. I'd also tell it to never invent a currency from a symbol alone unless the text makes it unambiguous.\\n\\nTo make the output safe for a billing system, I'd combine the prompt with structural enforcement rather than relying on the prompt alone. If the API supports JSON mode or a schema-constrained output, I'd use it. Downstream, I'd parse and validate every response against a schema before it reaches billing: types, the date format, the currency code list, and that total is a finite non-negative number. A response that fails validation gets one retry with the validation error included, then goes to a human review queue rather than into billing. I would never let malformed output pass through silently.\\n\\nFor reliability, I'd build a labeled eval set of messy invoices, including multi-currency documents, scanned-text noise, European number formats, missing totals, and prompt-injection text inside the invoice body. I'd grade on exact field match and on the rate of schema-valid output, and I'd track both per release. Before deploying I'd check that the null behavior works, because a confident wrong total is much more costly than a flagged missing one. In production I'd monitor the validation failure rate and the human-review rate, sample outputs regularly, and treat any change in model version as a trigger to rerun the full eval.\"},{\"id\":\"q3\",\"answer\":\"function solve(input) {\\n  const sep = /^---[ \\\\t]*$/m.exec(input);\\n  if (!sep) return input;\\n  let template = input.slice(0, sep.index);\\n  if (template.endsWith('\\\\n')) template = template.slice(0, -1);\\n  const raw = input.slice(sep.index + sep[0].length).trim();\\n  const values = raw === '' ? {} : JSON.parse(raw);\\n  return template.replace(/\\\\{\\\\{\\\\s*([^{}]+?)\\\\s*\\\\}\\\\}/g, (match, name) => {\\n    if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(values, name)) return match;\\n    const v = values[name];\\n    return typeof v === 'object' && v !== null ? JSON.stringify(v) : String(v);\\n  });\\n}\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":817,"output":2252,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0012077000000000001,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429024325},"c40c87c4ecd11c56a3b4e444a5047eaeef4299661f4b3a7a3f48e8789ba76ea8":{"response":{"text":"Five candidates now have verbatim evidence for all three must-haves: c154, c047, c006, c108, and c079. I'm requesting HR approval for this shortlist.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01R7pCsA6DwM3dGoB7jKexca","name":"request_hr_approval","input":{"reason":"Five candidates are fully evidenced for all must-haves, which meets the target of five.","candidate_ids":["c154","c047","c006","c108","c079"],"rationale":"All five have verbatim evidence for TypeScript (strict typing, zero any) and accessibility (WCAG 2.1 audits, screen-reader labels). React is a partial proxy for all five: each has a verbatim Next.js/Preact UI line, which is the accepted proxy from prior sessions, not explicit React project work. Nice-to-haves are covered: Storybook and shared UI kit work for design systems, and LCP/CLS work for web performance. Dropped: c155 (keyword-only profile, no real frontend work) and c056 (keyword-only, dropped in earlier session). Assessment phase should probe explicit React experience for all five."}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":44111,"output":447,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0046668000000000005,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429026421},"4bb269811df2a09beb3e24d9345b0399d9697861daf1afd3f3ec2321f4869b6d":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01NVCbc8wsHeryKbMcoDAt1u","name":"create_assessment","input":{"reason":"All five approved candidates have only partial React evidence (Next.js/Preact proxy), so React (m1) is the requirement I could not verify from their profiles.","candidates":[{"candidate_id":"c154","unverified_requirement_ids":["m1"]},{"candidate_id":"c047","unverified_requirement_ids":["m1"]},{"candidate_id":"c006","unverified_requirement_ids":["m1"]},{"candidate_id":"c108","unverified_requirement_ids":["m1"]},{"candidate_id":"c079","unverified_requirement_ids":["m1"]}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":46308,"output":372,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00484745,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429030341},"b57d47585705df7564fdbc8d99e2554f6f1605efeeccd141c0daf3982d1ae1fc":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"q1\",\"type\":\"mcq\",\"prompt\":\"In Pydantic v2, which approach validates an incoming request payload against a typed model and raises a structured validation error on invalid input?\",\"skills\":[\"Pydantic\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":true,\"options\":[\"Declare a BaseModel subclass with typed fields and parse the input with Model.model_validate()\",\"Use dict.get with default values and skip type checks\",\"Declare every field as Any and convert it with str()\",\"Pass the raw body through json.loads and do not validate it\"],\"answerKey\":\"Declare a BaseModel subclass with typed fields and parse the input with Model.model_validate()\",\"claimIds\":[\"skill-009\"],\"estimatedMinutes\":5},{\"id\":\"q2\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"Design a FastAPI ingestion endpoint that accepts batches of events and processes them with asyncio workers. Describe the request model, how you bound concurrency and queue size, how you handle a partially failed batch, and how the endpoint responds while work is still in progress.\",\"skills\":[\"FastAPI\",\"asyncio\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":true,\"rubric\":\"Score out of 4. (1) Typed Pydantic request model with batch size limits (1 point). (2) Bounded asyncio.Queue or semaphore to cap concurrency and apply backpressure, with a worker pool started and shut down via lifespan or equivalent (1 point). (3) Per-item success/failure reporting so a partial failure does not silently drop or fail the whole batch, with a clear strategy such as retries or a dead-letter path (1 point). (4) Response semantics such as 202 Accepted with a job or status identifier, and an explanation of how callers poll or receive results (1 point). Deduct for blocking calls inside async code and for no discussion of shutdown or cancellation.\",\"claimIds\":[\"skill-006\",\"skill-008\"],\"estimatedMinutes\":15},{\"id\":\"q3\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"You own the contract for a GET /orders query endpoint that supports filtering by status and date range, with pagination. Specify the query parameters, the response shape including pagination metadata, the error response format and status codes you would return for invalid filters, and how you would version the contract when a breaking change is needed.\",\"skills\":[\"REST API design\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":true,\"rubric\":\"Score out of 4. (1) Clear filter and pagination parameters with defaults and limits, such as cursor or limit/offset, with justification (1 point). (2) Response envelope with items plus pagination metadata, and correct status codes including 400 or 422 for invalid filters (1 point). (3) Consistent error format, such as RFC 9457 problem details or equivalent, with machine-readable codes (1 point). (4) A concrete versioning strategy such as URL or header versioning plus a deprecation policy for breaking changes (1 point). Deduct for using 200 for errors or for no reasoning about breaking changes.\",\"claimIds\":[\"skill-003\"],\"estimatedMinutes\":12}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":841,"output":1647,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0009076,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429031187},"ff75745cbd6f312c5f3e5fd8cf82a641aa2501327e09b1ee6795361d17cad402":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"id\":\"q1\",\"answer\":\"Two likely causes:\\n\\n1. Chunking boundaries split the relevant passage. Policy text often has the answer spanning a heading and its following paragraphs, so fixed-size chunks cut the key sentence away from its context. Change: switch to heading-aware chunking with a modest overlap (around 10-15%) so each chunk keeps its section title.\\n\\n2. Embedding-only retrieval misses exact terms like policy IDs or acronyms. Change: add a BM25 keyword pass and merge results with the vector results (hybrid retrieval), then rerank the top candidates.\\n\\nI'd check both by replaying the reported bad queries against the held-out set and confirming the correct chunk appears in the top 5.\"},{\"id\":\"q2\",\"answer\":\"Prompt changes: I'd state the output contract explicitly, saying to return only a JSON object with keys vendor, date, total, currency, and no other text. I'd give the schema and one example. For missing values, I'd instruct the model to return null rather than guess, and to never infer a total from line items unless a total label is present.\\n\\nCode changes: call the model with JSON mode or a schema-constrained output if the provider supports it. Otherwise, extract the first {...} block with a parser and validate it. If parsing fails, retry once with the error message appended. Validate that total is either null or a number, and that currency is a three-letter code. Route nulls to human review rather than filling them in.\\n\\nVerification: build a labeled set of about 100 OCR samples, including ones with no total, and track field-level accuracy, a hallucinated-total rate, and the prose-wrapping rate before and after the change. Gate the release on the hallucination rate not increasing.\"},{\"id\":\"q3\",\"answer\":\"function solve(input) {\\n  const idx = input.indexOf('\\\\nVARS:');\\n  const template = input.slice(0, idx);\\n  const vars = JSON.parse(input.slice(idx + 6));\\n  return template.replace(/\\\\{\\\\{(\\\\w+)\\\\}\\\\}/g, (m, k) => (k in vars ? vars[k] : m));\\n}\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":905,"output":1322,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0007515,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429031553},"15e1567ae361544fca193d6bf190432835fa22eb4ce56230138c37ff4ed22df3":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"claim-001\",\"skill\":\"Accessibility auditing (WCAG 2.1)\",\"assertion\":\"Ran WCAG 2.1 audits with screen readers and fixed focus traps across the checkout flow.\",\"evidence\":\"Ran WCAG 2.1 audits with screen readers and fixed focus traps across the checkout flow\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-002\",\"skill\":\"Design systems and shared UI kits\",\"assertion\":\"Shipped a shared UI kit with theme tokens adopted by six product squads.\",\"evidence\":\"Shipped a shared UI kit with theme tokens adopted by six product squads\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-003\",\"skill\":\"Storybook\",\"assertion\":\"Wrote Storybook docs for every component in the shared UI kit.\",\"evidence\":\"with Storybook docs for every piece\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-004\",\"skill\":\"TypeScript\",\"assertion\":\"Migrated a JavaScript codebase to strict typing with zero any escapes.\",\"evidence\":\"Migrated a JavaScript codebase to strict typing with zero any escapes\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-005\",\"skill\":\"Web performance (Largest Contentful Paint)\",\"assertion\":\"Brought Largest Contentful Paint under 1.5s on the marketing site by code-splitting and image work.\",\"evidence\":\"Brought Largest Contentful Paint under 1.5s on the marketing site by code-splitting and image work.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-006\",\"skill\":\"Web performance (Cumulative Layout Shift)\",\"assertion\":\"Has worked on Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) improvements.\",\"evidence\":\"Builder focused on lcp and cls work.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-007\",\"skill\":\"Next.js\",\"assertion\":\"Has built UIs with Next.js.\",\"evidence\":\"Next.js and Preact UIs\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-008\",\"skill\":\"Preact\",\"assertion\":\"Has built UIs with Preact.\",\"evidence\":\"Next.js and Preact UIs\",\"verified\":false}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":588,"output":1443,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0007802999999999999,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429035650},"59b658f82275a7145b353b472d0e4f4d07a9c137155e7f3807b983af149a6b66":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"id\":\"q1\",\"answer\":\"I would add a system instruction telling the model to only state refund policies that appear in the provided policy text, and to say it doesn't know if the policy isn't there. I would also include the actual refund policy text in the context window each turn. To check that these help, I would run the assistant on a set of refund questions and count how often it makes up a policy, comparing before and after the change.\"},{\"id\":\"q2\",\"answer\":\"For the repeated search loop, I would look at the traces to see whether the queries are actually changing. If they're nearly identical, I would add a rule in the prompt to reuse earlier results and a cap on the number of search calls. For giving up after one failure, I would make the prompt tell the agent to retry with a rephrased query when a search fails. In production I would add a maximum step count and a timeout so the agent stops after a set number of tool calls.\"},{\"id\":\"q3\",\"answer\":\"function solve(input) {\\n  const lines = input.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\");\\n  const template = lines[0];\\n  const vars = {};\\n  for (const line of lines.slice(1)) {\\n    const [k, v] = line.split(\\\"=\\\");\\n    vars[k.trim()] = v.trim();\\n  }\\n  return template.replace(/\\\\{\\\\{(\\\\w+)\\\\}\\\\}/, (m, k) => (k in vars ? vars[k] : m));\\n}\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":851,"output":917,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0005436,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429036689},"f86bea3d03451036509cb2a0f73d32a0a8cb275b5b38102c46b91b0c9f9646d7":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"fastapi-q1\",\"type\":\"mcq\",\"prompt\":\"In FastAPI, you define a path operation as `@app.get('/items/{item_id}')` with the function signature `def read_item(item_id: int):`. What does the `int` type hint do?\",\"skills\":[\"FastAPI\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"options\":[\"It only documents the parameter in the docs and performs no runtime checks.\",\"It automatically validates and converts the path parameter to an integer, returning a 422 error if conversion fails.\",\"It casts any value to an integer and silently defaults to 0 on failure.\",\"It makes the parameter optional with a default value of 0.\"],\"answerKey\":\"It automatically validates and converts the path parameter to an integer, returning a 422 error if conversion fails.\",\"estimatedMinutes\":6,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-003\"]},{\"id\":\"fastapi-q2\",\"type\":\"mcq\",\"prompt\":\"You need to share a database session across several FastAPI path operations and ensure it is closed after each request. Which FastAPI mechanism is the idiomatic choice?\",\"skills\":[\"FastAPI\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"options\":[\"Storing the session in a global variable initialized at import time.\",\"Dependency injection via Depends() with a generator dependency that yields the session.\",\"Passing the session as a query parameter from the client.\",\"Creating a new middleware class that stores the session on the request body.\"],\"answerKey\":\"Dependency injection via Depends() with a generator dependency that yields the session.\",\"estimatedMinutes\":6,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-003\"]},{\"id\":\"fastapi-q3\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"Describe how you would build a FastAPI endpoint that creates an order. Cover: (1) request validation using Pydantic models, (2) asynchronous database access, (3) how you would return appropriate HTTP status codes and handle a failure such as a duplicate order, and (4) how you would test the endpoint. Keep your answer under 300 words.\",\"skills\":[\"FastAPI\"],\"weight\":2,\"required\":false,\"rubric\":\"Score out of 10. Pydantic (0-2): uses a BaseModel for the request body with typed fields and mentions validation errors returning 422. Async DB (0-2): uses async def and an async driver or session, and notes not blocking the event loop. Error handling (0-3): returns 201 on success, uses HTTPException or a handler for conflicts (409) with a clear message, and avoids leaking internals. Testing (0-3): mentions TestClient or httpx AsyncClient, overriding dependencies (e.g., Depends override) to use a test database, and covers both success and failure cases. Deduct points for vague answers with no concrete FastAPI constructs.\",\"estimatedMinutes\":15,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-003\"]}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":708,"output":1447,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0007943,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429039040},"d9b6b2ff5f795b526e33ea599882d3218435d8ca11db1ae862461f670a364da4":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"claim-001\",\"skill\":\"Next.js\",\"assertion\":\"Works as a practitioner building UIs with Next.js.\",\"evidence\":\"Practitioner working on next.js and preact uis.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-002\",\"skill\":\"Preact\",\"assertion\":\"Works as a practitioner building UIs with Preact.\",\"evidence\":\"Practitioner working on next.js and preact uis.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-003\",\"skill\":\"Core Web Vitals (LCP)\",\"assertion\":\"Reduced Largest Contentful Paint to under 1.5s on the marketing site through code-splitting and image optimization.\",\"evidence\":\"Brought Largest Contentful Paint under 1.5s on the marketing site by code-splitting and image work.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-004\",\"skill\":\"Core Web Vitals (CLS)\",\"assertion\":\"Has worked on Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) performance.\",\"evidence\":\"They would describe their strengths as: LCP and CLS work\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-005\",\"skill\":\"WCAG accessibility auditing\",\"assertion\":\"Ran WCAG 2.1 accessibility audits and fixed focus traps across the checkout flow.\",\"evidence\":\"Ran WCAG 2.1 audits with screen readers and fixed focus traps across the checkout flow.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-006\",\"skill\":\"Screen-reader testing\",\"assertion\":\"Tested interfaces with screen readers during accessibility audits.\",\"evidence\":\"Ran WCAG 2.1 audits with screen readers\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-007\",\"skill\":\"Strict TypeScript\",\"assertion\":\"Rebuilt a component kit under strict static typing with zero use of 'any'.\",\"evidence\":\"Rebuilt the component kit under strict static typing (tsc --strict, zero any)\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-008\",\"skill\":\"ARIA and keyboard accessibility\",\"assertion\":\"Implemented ARIA roles, keyboard navigation and screen-reader labels on every control in a component kit.\",\"evidence\":\"with ARIA roles, keyboard navigation and screen-reader labels on every control.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-009\",\"skill\":\"Design systems\",\"assertion\":\"Shipped a shared UI kit with theme tokens adopted by six product squads.\",\"evidence\":\"Shipped a shared UI kit with theme tokens adopted by six product squads\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-010\",\"skill\":\"Storybook\",\"assertion\":\"Documented every component of a shared UI kit with Storybook.\",\"evidence\":\"with Storybook docs for every piece.\",\"verified\":false}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":621,"output":1538,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0008311,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429041435},"5a494bc9d0aa03c8715ee7b191ecc965eef5980e9cfc4a3eb1b4dfe7f2b1c1c4":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"id\":\"q1\",\"answer\":\"I would add a line to the system prompt telling the model not to make up policies and to say it doesn't know if unsure. I would also add a few example refund questions with good answers to the prompt. To check whether it helped, I would try some refund questions and see if the answers look better.\"},{\"id\":\"q2\",\"answer\":\"If it loops, I would add a rule in the prompt saying not to repeat searches, and maybe cap the number of tool calls. If it gives up early, I would tell it to try harder before answering. For production, I would set a maximum number of steps and add a timeout so it doesn't run forever. I would look at logs to see what went wrong.\"},{\"id\":\"q3\",\"answer\":\"```javascript\\nfunction solve(input) {\\n  const lines = input.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\");\\n  const template = lines[0];\\n  const vars = {};\\n  for (let i = 1; i < lines.length; i++) {\\n    const line = lines[i].trim();\\n    if (!line) continue;\\n    const parts = line.split(\\\"=\\\");\\n    vars[parts[0]] = parts[1];\\n  }\\n  return template.replace(/\\\\{\\\\{(\\\\w+)\\\\}\\\\}/g, (m, name) =>\\n    name in vars ? vars[name] : m\\n  );\\n}\\n```\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":859,"output":941,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0005564,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429041484},"bbef4bde4c12d6c658ff46375354023f7a5899b7dbd7a03e98ed3614f7864484":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"claim-001\",\"skill\":\"Next.js\",\"assertion\":\"Builds user interfaces with Next.js\",\"evidence\":\"Practitioner working on next.js and preact uis.\"},{\"id\":\"claim-002\",\"skill\":\"Preact\",\"assertion\":\"Builds user interfaces with Preact\",\"evidence\":\"Practitioner working on next.js and preact uis.\"},{\"id\":\"claim-003\",\"skill\":\"Storybook\",\"assertion\":\"Documented UI components with Storybook\",\"evidence\":\"with Storybook docs for every piece\"},{\"id\":\"claim-004\",\"skill\":\"TypeScript\",\"assertion\":\"Rebuilt a component kit under strict static typing with zero use of any\",\"evidence\":\"Rebuilt the component kit under strict static typing (tsc --strict, zero any)\"},{\"id\":\"claim-005\",\"skill\":\"Accessibility (ARIA)\",\"assertion\":\"Implemented ARIA roles, keyboard navigation and screen-reader labels on every control in the component kit\",\"evidence\":\"with ARIA roles, keyboard navigation and screen-reader labels on every control\"},{\"id\":\"claim-006\",\"skill\":\"WCAG auditing\",\"assertion\":\"Ran WCAG 2.1 accessibility audits using screen readers\",\"evidence\":\"Ran WCAG 2.1 audits with screen readers and fixed focus traps across the checkout flow\"},{\"id\":\"claim-007\",\"skill\":\"Accessibility remediation (focus management)\",\"assertion\":\"Fixed focus traps across the checkout flow\",\"evidence\":\"fixed focus traps across the checkout flow\"},{\"id\":\"claim-008\",\"skill\":\"Design systems\",\"assertion\":\"Shipped a shared UI kit with theme tokens adopted by six product squads\",\"evidence\":\"Shipped a shared UI kit with theme tokens adopted by six product squads\"},{\"id\":\"claim-009\",\"skill\":\"Web performance optimization\",\"assertion\":\"Reduced Largest Contentful Paint to under 1.5s on the marketing site using code-splitting and image work\",\"evidence\":\"Brought Largest Contentful Paint under 1.5s on the marketing site by code-splitting and image work.\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":634,"output":1204,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0006654,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429046158},"2466464a6be12304bf93d3cc6d659204924e68f714ad90d4c6f46a02b132bc12":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"q1\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"You have a labelled evaluation set of query-to-relevant-passage pairs and an HNSW index for dense retrieval. Explain how you would tune HNSW parameters (M, efConstruction, efSearch) to improve recall@10 while keeping p95 query latency within budget. Describe how you would measure recall against the labelled set and what trade-offs you would watch for.\",\"skills\":[\"HNSW indexing\",\"retrieval recall tuning\"],\"required\":true,\"rubric\":\"Score 0-4. +1 explains that M and efConstruction affect index build time, memory, and graph quality, while efSearch controls the query-time recall/latency trade-off. +1 defines recall@k against the labelled set (fraction of relevant items found in top-k) and describes a sweep over parameters. +1 names a concrete trade-off, such as memory growth with M or latency growth with efSearch, and explains how to pick a point on the curve. +1 mentions a validation step, such as holding out queries, checking for overfitting to the eval set, or comparing against exact brute-force search as a baseline.\",\"claimIds\":[\"skill-002\",\"skill-003\"],\"estimatedMinutes\":12},{\"id\":\"q2\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"A model-serving endpoint has p95 lookup latency of 900ms. Describe the steps you would take to reduce p95 to under 100ms, covering batching of inference requests and caching of repeated lookups. Explain how you would confirm each change helped.\",\"skills\":[\"lookup latency optimization\",\"batched inference and caching\"],\"required\":true,\"rubric\":\"Score 0-4. +1 starts with profiling or tracing to locate where time is spent before changing code. +1 explains dynamic or micro-batching of inference requests, including the trade-off between batch size, wait time, and tail latency. +1 describes a cache design for repeated lookups, including key choice, warm-up, eviction, and invalidation. +1 specifies measurement: p95 (not only mean) under a realistic load test, with before/after comparisons for each change.\",\"claimIds\":[\"skill-005\",\"skill-011\"],\"estimatedMinutes\":12},{\"id\":\"q3\",\"type\":\"mcq\",\"prompt\":\"You need to serve approximate nearest-neighbour search over 40 million product description embeddings with FAISS on a single machine with limited RAM. Which approach is most appropriate?\",\"skills\":[\"FAISS\"],\"options\":[\"Use a flat exact index (IndexFlatL2) holding all raw float32 vectors in memory\",\"Use an IVF index with product quantization (for example IVFPQ) trained on a sample of the vectors\",\"Store each embedding as a row in a relational table and compute distances with a SQL full scan per query\",\"Use a random-projection hash table with one bucket per vector\"],\"answerKey\":\"Use an IVF index with product quantization (for example IVFPQ) trained on a sample of the vectors\",\"claimIds\":[\"skill-004\"],\"estimatedMinutes\":5}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":1274,"output":1443,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0008489,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429046952},"23c9f8565151b5764436bd2ad3c97925d0ecfc038277d7d67485512437b97994":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"id\":\"q1\",\"answer\":\"I would add a line to the system prompt telling the model not to make up policies and to say it doesn't know if unsure. I would also add a few example refund questions with good answers to the prompt. To check whether it helped, I would try some refund questions and see if the answers look better.\"},{\"id\":\"q2\",\"answer\":\"If it loops, I would add a rule in the prompt saying not to repeat searches, and maybe cap the number of tool calls. If it gives up early, I would tell it to try harder before answering. For production, I would set a maximum number of steps and add a timeout so it doesn't run forever. I would look at logs to see what went wrong.\"},{\"id\":\"q3\",\"answer\":\"function solve(input) {\\n  const lines = input.split(String.fromCharCode(10));\\n  let out = lines[0];\\n  for (let i = 1; i < lines.length; i++) {\\n    const line = lines[i].trim();\\n    if (line.length === 0) continue;\\n    const idx = line.indexOf(\\\"=\\\");\\n    const name = line.slice(0, idx);\\n    const value = line.slice(idx + 1);\\n    out = out.split(\\\"{{\\\" + name + \\\"}}\\\").join(value);\\n  }\\n  return out;\\n}\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":1338,"output":1646,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0009568,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429049196},"9b5106de1463e871a1f567a3bb57810a45f8ddbc084317eb06772c2f74b5a583":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"q-001\",\"type\":\"mcq\",\"prompt\":\"A candidate reports cutting p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms with a FAISS index. Which factor most directly determines whether that result can be reproduced on a similar workload?\",\"skills\":[\"FAISS\",\"Search latency optimization\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":true,\"answerKey\":\"Whether the index type, its parameters (for example nlist/nprobe or M/efSearch), and the recall target were matched to the query load and corpus size, with latency measured at the same percentile and recall level\",\"options\":[\"Whether the index type, its parameters (for example nlist/nprobe or M/efSearch), and the recall target were matched to the query load and corpus size, with latency measured at the same percentile and recall level\",\"Whether the service runs on GPUs rather than CPUs\",\"Whether the vectors were normalized to unit length before indexing\",\"Whether the API layer is written in FastAPI or Flask\"],\"claimIds\":[\"claim-011\",\"claim-012\"],\"estimatedMinutes\":10},{\"id\":\"q-002\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"You are serving a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI endpoint. Describe how you would implement batched inference so that concurrent requests are grouped without exceeding a latency budget. Cover: how batches are formed (size and wait-time limits), how results are returned to each request, and how you would handle a single slow or malformed request in a batch.\",\"skills\":[\"Batched inference\",\"FastAPI\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":true,\"rubric\":\"Score 0-4. 1 point each for: (a) batch formation with both a max batch size and a max wait time; (b) a queue or asyncio mechanism that maps each result back to its originating request future; (c) validating inputs before batching or isolating a malformed item so it does not fail the whole batch; (d) noting that the model call should not block the event loop (run in an executor or a dedicated worker) and that the answer addresses the latency budget tradeoff.\",\"skills_note\":\"\",\"claimIds\":[\"claim-003\",\"claim-006\"],\"estimatedMinutes\":15},{\"id\":\"q-003\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"Three internal teams depend on a passage ranking service. Describe the REST contract you would define for a query endpoint, including request and response shapes, how you would version it, and how Pydantic models would enforce those shapes at the boundary. Include how you would make breaking changes safe for existing consumers.\",\"skills\":[\"REST API design\",\"Pydantic\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":true,\"rubric\":\"Score 0-4. 1 point each for: (a) resource and endpoint design with clear request and response fields, including pagination or top-k limits; (b) explicit versioning strategy (URL or header) with a stated rule for what counts as a breaking change; (c) Pydantic models for request and response with validation constraints (types, ranges, required vs optional) and separate models for input and output; (d) a concrete migration approach such as deprecation periods, additive-only changes, or contract tests with consumers.\",\"claimIds\":[\"claim-001\",\"claim-005\"],\"estimatedMinutes\":15}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":1259,"output":1466,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0008589,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429055094},"651f476b3a62d93e435f8c03871e4cb00df4c29a58a27fae89a98e440359f535":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01QZwy5rQJfbQcPE5mUTT3rV","name":"send_assessment","input":{"reason":"Five assessments were generated targeting each candidate's unverified nice-to-haves, and all five candidates are HR-approved, so the gate allows sending.","candidate_ids":["c049","c156","c066","c122","c002"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":36494,"output":144,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0037520500000000003,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429056425},"b63acfe576ea7e57993f128ecf7f664efc332b441877ae0c0e7d4d15498b22e3":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_011wYWVePfGdV4q862fhHCtD","name":"collect_responses","input":{"reason":"Assessments were sent to all five approved candidates; collecting their answers so they can be graded against the rubric and the code tests.","candidate_ids":["c049","c156","c066","c122","c002"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":38696,"output":134,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00396725,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429057535},"c9b2b1f3f46b072ab1fd2db822caa792f720212cddb0fe993616b70b6721f43a":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"id\":\"q-001\",\"answer\":\"Declare Pydantic models as endpoint parameter and response types, and call a service-layer function from the route handler\"},{\"id\":\"q-002\",\"answer\":\"I'd run a fixed pool of N worker tasks (N=10) that all pull from the same asyncio.Queue, using an asyncio.Semaphore(10) to cap in-flight work so the pool never exceeds the limit. Each worker loops on await queue.get(), processes the job inside try/except so one bad job logs and moves on instead of killing the worker, and I'd put queue.task_done() in the finally block. For shutdown, I'd catch CancelledError at the top of the worker, stop taking new jobs, and drain whatever is already in flight with asyncio.gather before exiting, so nothing gets dropped silently. Roughly:\\n\\nasync def worker(q, sem):\\n    while True:\\n        job = await q.get()\\n        try:\\n            async with sem:\\n                await process(job)\\n        except Exception:\\n            log.exception('job failed')\\n        q.task_done()\\n\\nFor the cancel path I'd also persist unfinished jobs back to a dead-letter store rather than rely on memory, since a cancel mid-process can still lose work. I haven't stress-tested the shutdown path under load, so I'd add a test for that.\"},{\"id\":\"q-003\",\"answer\":\"I treat the OpenAPI spec as the source of truth and check it into the repo, generated from the FastAPI/Pydantic models and reviewed in PRs. To catch breaking changes before release, I run a diff tool like oasdiff in CI against the last released spec, and fail the build on removed endpoints, changed required fields, narrowed types, or new required request params. For a change that must break clients, I'd ship it as a new version (for example /v2 under a new route prefix) and keep /v1 running. I'd mark the old version deprecated in the spec, send a Deprecation and Sunset header on responses, announce the timeline to internal client owners with a migration guide, and track which clients still call v1 through request logs before removing it. I'd also aim to make additive changes instead of breaking ones wherever possible.\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":911,"output":1237,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0007096,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429064488},"357d2418310ec9c7dbf72997ca6732dd9c3decb2701ea58eef400aa62395a71a":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"id\":\"q1\",\"answer\":\"Declare a BaseModel subclass with typed fields and parse the input with Model.model_validate()\"},{\"id\":\"q2\",\"answer\":\"Request model: EventBatch(BaseModel) with batch_id: str, events: list[Event] constrained by Field(min_length=1, max_length=500), and Event carrying a typed type, a timezone-aware occurred_at, and a payload dict with a size limit. Validation happens at the boundary, so malformed events are rejected with a 422 before any work is queued.\\n\\nConcurrency and backpressure: the endpoint puts validated events onto a bounded asyncio.Queue (maxsize around 10x worker count). A fixed pool of N worker tasks, created at startup and cancelled on shutdown, drains the queue with an asyncio.Semaphore capping in-flight downstream calls. If the queue is full, the endpoint returns 429 with Retry-After instead of buffering without limit. Each request also has a timeout so a slow downstream cannot hold the connection open.\\n\\nPartial failure: each event is processed independently and its outcome is recorded per event_id, not per batch. Transient errors are retried with exponential backoff and jitter up to a small cap; permanent errors (schema or business-rule failures) are routed to a dead-letter store with the reason. Processing is idempotent on event_id so retries and redeliveries do not double-apply.\\n\\nResponse while work is in progress: the endpoint returns 202 Accepted with a batch_id and a status URL such as GET /batches/{batch_id}, which reports queued, processing, completed, or completed_with_failures and counts per outcome. Clients poll or receive a webhook. The endpoint never waits for the whole batch to finish, so latency stays flat regardless of batch size.\"},{\"id\":\"q3\",\"answer\":\"Query parameters: status (repeatable enum: pending, paid, shipped, cancelled), created_from and created_to (ISO 8601 dates, inclusive, UTC; created_from must not be after created_to), limit (integer, default 50, max 200), and an opaque cursor for pagination. Cursor-based pagination is preferred over offset so results stay stable under inserts.\\n\\nResponse shape: {\\\"data\\\": [Order...], \\\"pagination\\\": {\\\"next_cursor\\\": string|null, \\\"has_more\\\": boolean, \\\"limit\\\": integer}}. Each Order has id, status, created_at, total, and currency. Field names and types are fixed in a documented schema.\\n\\nErrors: a single envelope for all failures: {\\\"error\\\": {\\\"code\\\": string, \\\"message\\\": string, \\\"details\\\": [{\\\"field\\\": string, \\\"reason\\\": string}]}}. Invalid filters return 400 with codes such as invalid_status or invalid_date_range, and each offending field listed in details. A malformed or tampered cursor returns 400 invalid_cursor. Unknown or unauthorized resources return 401 or 403; a server fault returns 500 with a request id and no internal detail. Validation of unknown query parameters is explicit so typos fail loudly rather than being ignored.\\n\\nVersioning: the path carries the major version (/v1/orders, later /v2/orders). Additive changes such as a new optional field or a new enum value that clients can ignore stay in v1 and are documented in a changelog. A breaking change, such as renaming a field, changing a type, or altering pagination semantics, ships under /v2. v1 is kept alive with a Deprecation header and a Sunset date, with at least one deprecation cycle and usage monitoring before removal. Contract tests run against both versions in CI so the behaviour promised to consumers is enforced.\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":855,"output":1539,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.000855,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429072897},"40c61ff1a3a6ed3327649c86f328ef0ff358c999f16580db678d75cc84ae585f":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"id\":\"q1\",\"answer\":\"I would tune HNSW in stages, starting from a baseline. M sets graph connectivity: higher M improves recall on hard queries but increases memory and build time, so I would sweep a small range such as 16, 32, and 48 and watch index size. efConstruction controls build-time search breadth; raising it improves graph quality and recall at the cost of build time, and it has no effect on query latency. efSearch is the main runtime knob: it trades query latency for recall, so I would sweep it (for example 32, 64, 128, 256) with the other parameters held fixed. To measure recall, I would run every labelled query through both the HNSW index and an exact brute-force search over the same vectors to get ground truth, then compute recall@10 as the fraction of true top-10 relevant passages that appear in the HNSW top-10. I would also report hit rate against the labelled relevant passages, since exact-neighbour recall and relevance can diverge. For latency, I would measure p50, p95 and p99 under a realistic concurrency level and with warm and cold caches, not a single-threaded microbenchmark. Trade-offs to watch: diminishing returns in recall past a certain efSearch, memory growth with M, rebuild cost when the corpus changes, and filtered queries where post-filtering can silently drop recall. I would pick the lowest efSearch that meets the recall target with p95 inside budget, and re-validate after every corpus refresh.\"},{\"id\":\"q2\",\"answer\":\"I would start by profiling where the 900ms goes, splitting time between query encoding, index lookup, network hops, serialization, and queueing, since the fix depends on which dominates. For inference, I would enable dynamic request batching in the serving layer: collect incoming queries for a short window (a few milliseconds) or until a maximum batch size, then run one batched forward pass through the sentence encoder. I would tune the window and batch size against p95 rather than throughput alone, because larger windows add waiting time. For repeated lookups, I would add a cache keyed on a normalised query string or its embedding hash, with a TTL and size bound, and pre-warm it with the most frequent queries from logs. I would also keep the model loaded and warmed at startup to avoid first-request penalties, and check that the index is memory-resident rather than paged from disk. Each change would be measured in isolation against the same replayed production-like traffic: I would record p50, p95 and p99 latency, cache hit rate, batch size distribution, and error rate before and after, and keep a change only if p95 improves without hurting recall or correctness. I would also verify that cached and batched results match uncached single-request results on a sample, to confirm the optimisations do not change answers. Once the cache and batching are in place, I would check whether the remaining tail comes from index search parameters, which I would tune as described for HNSW, and then confirm the under-100ms target holds under peak concurrency with a load test.\"},{\"id\":\"q3\",\"answer\":\"Use an IVF index with product quantization (for example IVFPQ) trained on a sample of the vectors\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":913,"output":1146,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0006642999999999999,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429079870},"f0666e980a3937d533dcd20907aaf7dee3098d17e9af9339a44d9d77712df392":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"id\":\"q-001\",\"answer\":\"Whether the service runs on GPUs rather than CPUs\"},{\"id\":\"q-002\",\"answer\":\"I'd use an async queue in FastAPI and group requests into a batch so the model runs fewer times. Something like a batch size of around 32 and a short wait so requests don't sit too long. Then I'd split the outputs and send each one back to the request that sent it. For slow or bad requests I'd just let the batch run and handle errors if they come up.\"},{\"id\":\"q-003\",\"answer\":\"I'd make a POST /query endpoint that takes a query string and a top_k value and returns a list of passages with scores. I'd use Pydantic models for the request and response so the fields are typed. For versioning I'd put a version in the URL path like /v1/ and keep the old version running. For breaking changes I'd announce them to the teams ahead of time and try to avoid removing fields so existing consumers keep working.\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":938,"output":695,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0004413,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429084072},"53b8fe00b7cf5a38924925831722cf666d54b4113c9babd4724dd4cd89d6009b":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"id\":\"fastapi-q1\",\"answer\":\"It only documents the parameter in the docs and performs no runtime checks.\"},{\"id\":\"fastapi-q2\",\"answer\":\"Storing the session in a global variable initialized at import time.\"},{\"id\":\"fastapi-q3\",\"answer\":\"I'd make a Pydantic model for the order request so the body gets validated, then make the endpoint async and save the order to the database with an async session. For the response I'd return a 200 or 201 and the order data. If there's a duplicate order I'd catch the error and return an error response. For testing I'd call the endpoint with a test client and check that it returns the right result, and maybe add a few more tests if there's time.\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":958,"output":593,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0003923,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429090231},"25e437ba65bef23c7357406ef0837bfcd7c41179d23a672813ce97d982763066":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01LNiSqW9rf5yWB7LgEhmtoC","name":"run_code","input":{"reason":"Responses are collected for all five; running the JavaScript answers against the hidden tests so code results can check the rubric grades, since earlier runs failed with a harness error.","candidate_ids":["c049","c156","c066","c122","c002"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":41441,"output":145,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00424725,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429089631},"70fc002e84211ff8e9bf8890399de418bad43cd7d7d4b8710473fe022c1e1016":{"response":{"text":"The sandbox found no coding answers, because these assessments had no coding task. The grade will rest on the rubric, with the code component marked as not assessed.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_018nzarLUiXELRxC5qq3rQuX","name":"grade_answer","input":{"reason":"run_code returned no coding answers for any candidate, so the code component is absent and grading must rest on the rubric for the collected short and scenario answers.","candidate_ids":["c049","c156","c066","c122","c002"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":43373,"output":238,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00448695,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429091385},"9990067a55342567fa6170d3efdd42d67b540bd398cd7ea94acb3b019999b1a3":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01Lz759g7dHSbmmJUSWxchuo","name":"run_code","input":{"reason":"Responses are collected; the code answers need running against the hidden tests, since memory warns that rubric-only code grades were unreliable and earlier harness runs failed.","candidate_ids":["c049","c156","c066","c122","c002"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":41435,"output":144,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00424615,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429091448},"dc396a43b714b1fa22cbc8fb26d8962f000d54bf5316db1958a4a3a257721ed7":{"response":{"text":"The sandbox found no JavaScript answers to run, because the collected responses contain no code submission. The rubric grader will score the written answers, and I'll flag the code part as unverified.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01EyVZigD3fVGbbbvtVVQK92","name":"grade_answer","input":{"reason":"run_code reported no coding answer for any candidate, so the code tests cannot be used; grading the collected written answers against the rubric and marking the code component as unverified.","candidate_ids":["c049","c156","c066","c122","c002"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":43366,"output":261,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00449775,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429093494},"12f5b697f4ee72c58eb098bb6420ebb6aa469b71c1d160d934f7352729e9b707":{"response":{"text":"{\"score\":0.5,\"reason\":\"Covers bounded concurrency (fixed pool of 10 workers plus a Semaphore), per-job try/except so one failure doesn't kill the worker, and no event-loop blocking. Deductions: queue.task_done() is placed after the try/except rather than in a finally block, so it is skipped if CancelledError propagates. The answer says to catch CancelledError and drain with asyncio.gather but never states re-raising it, and catching it inside the worker then draining in-flight work is a muddled shutdown sequence. The gather-in-worker idea is also unclear since the worker itself is the in-flight task. Partial credit for the sound core design.\",\"confidence\":0.8}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":961,"output":581,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0003866,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429096756},"ab938be2d488e0b11104d7da98e2f6a08b404da695e7fc1a4ea04166356a8b7c":{"response":{"text":"{\"score\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Covers all four rubric elements: the OpenAPI spec is the checked-in source of truth, reviewed in PRs; oasdiff runs in CI against the last release to catch removed endpoints, changed required fields, narrowed types, and new required params; breaking changes ship as /v2 with /v1 kept alive, plus deprecation marking and Deprecation/Sunset headers; and clients get advance notice, a migration guide, and usage tracking. Minor gap: no explicit changelog is mentioned, so it is not a perfect 4/4 (score 3.6/4 equivalent).\",\"confidence\":0.85}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":759,"output":563,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00035739999999999996,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429099907},"546b73f6ea0a45c046094ca05bbc108d310424040bf3ea4c2fa5bdd06f2dfa19":{"response":{"text":"{\"score\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Covers all four rubric items. (1) Typed Pydantic EventBatch with Field(min_length=1, max_length=500) and typed Event fields. (2) Bounded asyncio.Queue plus a semaphore for concurrency, with workers created at startup and cancelled on shutdown, and 429 with Retry-After for backpressure. (3) Per-event outcomes, retries with backoff and jitter, and a dead-letter path with idempotency. (4) 202 Accepted with batch_id and a status URL, plus polling or webhook delivery. Minor deduction: shutdown is mentioned only as cancellation, with no discussion of draining in-flight work or handling cancellation of partially processed events. No blocking calls in async code were described, and none were introduced.\",\"confidence\":0.85}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":1090,"output":543,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0003805,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429102835},"23edb905d1ec371003cd850d5e6f2d13bbe9b1ecce69db4e48f2c394dd0565f3":{"response":{"text":"{\"score\":0.95,\"reason\":\"Meets all four rubric criteria. (1) Filters are specified (repeatable status enum, inclusive UTC created_from/created_to with ordering rule) with cursor pagination, a default limit of 50, a max of 200, and a stated justification for cursors over offset. (2) The envelope has a data array plus pagination metadata (next_cursor, has_more, limit), which is equivalent to items plus metadata, and invalid filters return 400. (3) A single error envelope with machine-readable codes (invalid_status, invalid_date_range, invalid_cursor) and per-field details. (4) Path-based major versioning, a rule for what counts as breaking, Deprecation and Sunset headers, a deprecation cycle, and CI contract tests. Minor flaw not covered by the rubric: unknown resources are given 401/403 where 404 would be more apt, which only slightly lowers the score.\",\"confidence\":0.9}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":1139,"output":615,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0004214,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429105977},"45c9243665f06a1fa7c30a2a6521895cc6cf36f28d007cc813cea618611b692a":{"response":{"text":"{\"score\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Covers all four rubric criteria. It explains that M and efConstruction affect build time, memory, and graph quality, while efSearch governs the query-time recall/latency trade-off. It defines recall@10 and describes sweeps over each parameter. It names concrete trade-offs (memory growth with M, latency growth and diminishing returns with efSearch) and picks the lowest efSearch meeting the recall target within the p95 budget. It validates against exact brute-force search as a baseline. Minor deduction: the primary recall is measured against brute-force neighbours rather than the labelled relevant passages, which the question asked for; the labelled hit rate is mentioned only as a secondary metric. It also does not discuss holdout or overfitting to the eval set.\",\"confidence\":0.85}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":1016,"output":667,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0004351,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429109341},"6e6a2976def44ce15cfb9b982b51b13eae0e7cb8018aa57432ca574fb4ea1e1e":{"response":{"text":"{\"score\":1.0,\"reason\":\"Meets all four criteria. It starts with profiling to locate where time is spent (encoding, index lookup, network, serialization, queueing). It describes dynamic batching with a wait window and max batch size, and notes that larger windows add waiting time, so tuning must target p95. The cache design covers key choice (normalised query or embedding hash), warm-up from logs, size-bounded eviction, and TTL. Measurement uses p50/p95/p99 under replayed production-like traffic, with before/after comparisons for each change in isolation. One minor gap: cache invalidation is handled only by TTL and does not address invalidating entries when the index or model changes, but the cache criterion is still substantially met.\",\"confidence\":0.85}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":937,"output":583,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0003852,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429112710},"6ff4273d1fc116474906b7ddf565f71b7f24802783201acd52e219d185e0fd60":{"response":{"text":"{\"score\":0.5,\"reason\":\"Meets (b): it names an async queue and says outputs are split and returned to the originating request. Partially meets (a): it gives a max batch size (~32) and a 'short wait', but the wait limit is vague and not tied to a latency budget. Fails (c): it says to 'let the batch run' on slow or malformed requests, with no validation before batching and no isolation of bad items. Fails (d): it never mentions running the model off the event loop (executor or worker), and its treatment of the latency tradeoff is a single phrase.\",\"confidence\":0.85}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":608,"output":537,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00032930000000000004,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429116082},"f4a067eba36590461350f165b95f254ade88d963e69e2fe893789e2d2cd6e948":{"response":{"text":"{\"score\":0.5,\"reason\":\"Criterion (a): Partly met. It names POST /query, a query string, top_k, and scored passages, but does not define the response fields or a bound on top_k. Criterion (b): Partly met. It states URL versioning (/v1/) and keeps old versions running, but gives no explicit rule for what counts as a breaking change. Criterion (c): Largely unmet. It says the fields are 'typed' but gives no validation constraints (ranges, required vs optional) and no separate input and output models. Criterion (d): Met. It describes advance announcement, keeping the old version running, and avoiding field removal, which amounts to a deprecation and additive-change approach. Overall about 2 of 4 points.\",\"confidence\":0.7}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":620,"output":680,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.000402,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429120003},"d0316ad6c0c9d0de380ffa25970cea35ef5755a0c70e1372e16cac140d14f6c0":{"response":{"text":"{\"score\":0.33,\"reason\":\"Pydantic (1/2): names a Pydantic model for the request body but gives no typed fields and no mention of 422 validation errors. Async DB (1/2): says the endpoint is async with an async session, but names no driver and does not mention avoiding event-loop blocking. Error handling (1/3): mentions catching a duplicate, but returns no specific status code (it says '200 or 201'), names no 409 or HTTPException, and gives no clear error message. Testing (1/3): mentions a test client, but no dependency override for a test database, and failure cases are only vaguely covered ('a few more tests if there's time'). Overall the answer is vague and lacks concrete FastAPI constructs, so it earns about 3.5 of 10.\",\"confidence\":0.85}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":693,"output":572,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0003553,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429126713},"1e1dd9efae5cb76db6464d386d7d46b5df7aa3f8f69023c1204e37bd610085ae":{"response":{"text":"All five approved candidates now have graded assessments, and the grader raised no flags that call for a regrade. Advancing to AUDIT.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01EXgyAntmxEQBtjX2F4JYqG","name":"advance_phase","input":{"reason":"All five HR-approved candidates have graded assessments (c066 0.97, c156 0.95, c049 0.80, c122 0.33, c002 0.18), and none were flagged for regrade, so the ASSESS exit condition holds."}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":47189,"output":304,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00490155,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429125535},"79975d92ecd4cf72ffa02b2eb49f8189c396d19df9aeb8005fcf8e36a4c10f9a":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_018fYG7idqgfBtBM6v7qvNbm","name":"fairness_audit","input":{"reason":"Advancing to AUDIT: the live shortlist is the five HR-approved, assessed candidates, and I need the identity-redacted rerank to check for rank instability and top-5 overlap.","shortlist_ids":["c049","c156","c066","c122","c002"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":49056,"output":159,"cacheRead":2519,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00501029,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts