models that reconcile the finance and product marts\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"skill-006\",\"skill\":\"Snowflake data modelling\",\"assertion\":\"Modelled a Snowflake warehouse with dbt, with tests on every mart\",\"years\":null,\"evidence\":\"Modelled a Snowflake warehouse with dbt, with tests on every mart.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"skill-007\",\"skill\":\"BigQuery data modelling\",\"assertion\":\"Has modelled data in BigQuery\",\"years\":null,\"evidence\":\"Snowflake and BigQuery modelling\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"skill-008\",\"skill\":\"SQL window functions\",\"assertion\":\"Wrote window-function queries\",\"years\":null,\"evidence\":\"Wrote the window-function queries\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"skill-009\",\"skill\":\"DAG orchestration\",\"assertion\":\"Has experience with DAG orchestration\",\"years\":null,\"evidence\":\"DAG orchestration\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"skill-010\",\"skill\":\"Kafka\",\"assertion\":\"Wrote Kafka consumers that land clickstream events in BigQuery within a minute\",\"years\":null,\"evidence\":\"Wrote Kafka consumers that land clickstream events in BigQuery within a minute.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"skill-011\",\"skill\":\"Overall professional experience\",\"assertion\":\"Has 7 years of professional experience\",\"years\":7,\"evidence\":\"has 7 years of experience\",\"verified\":false}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":596,"output":1666,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0008926,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429260081},"f16371c246b9cde92c31d453633a7cdea2b5486a77ba0e64a5b0bc6b549f8d5a":{"response":{"text":"Three candidates are now fully evidenced: Kunal Joshi, Ravi Bose and Ivan Petrov. Nora Sharma (c020) is the last previously approved candidate, so I'll open her profile next.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_013fmyaHYrgta2iqVAxd6MFp","name":"get_profile","input":{"reason":"Nora Sharma (c020) was previously approved in memory and is not rejected; her profile is needed to check must-have evidence for the fourth shortlist slot.","candidate_id":"c020"}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":19883,"output":243,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.002142,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429260456},"cc188b0e30c112c9520e0eb6ee6d393a992e9d822fb4d31e0da3fce3f3637697":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\": [{\"id\":\"q-ts-strict-001\",\"type\":\"mcq\",\"prompt\":\"You are migrating a JavaScript module to TypeScript with `strict` enabled. A third-party library returns data whose shape you cannot determine at compile time. Which approach best avoids introducing `any` escapes?\",\"skills\":[\"TypeScript\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":true,\"options\":[\"Annotate the value as `any` and add a comment explaining why\",\"Use `// @ts-ignore` above the call site so the build passes\",\"Type the value as `unknown` and narrow it with type guards or a schema validator before use\",\"Cast the result with `as any` and rely on runtime tests to catch errors\"],\"answerKey\":\"Type the value as `unknown` and narrow it with type guards or a schema validator before use\",\"estimatedMinutes\":5,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-004\"]},{\"id\":\"q-nextjs-ui-001\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"Describe how you would build a product listing page in Next.js that loads data for the page, and explain the difference between rendering it on the server and rendering it on the client. Name one concrete trade-off of each.\",\"skills\":[\"Next.js\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":true,\"rubric\":\"Score out of 4. 1 point: correctly identifies a Next.js data-fetching approach for the page (e.g., Server Components with async fetch, getServerSideProps, or the App Router equivalent). 1 point: accurately explains server rendering (HTML generated on the server, sent to the browser). 1 point: accurately explains client rendering (HTML shell then JavaScript fetches and renders, or 'use client' components). 1 point: names one valid trade-off for each approach (e.g., server: fresher data and SEO versus higher server load or latency; client: lighter server work and interactivity versus slower first paint and SEO risk). Deduct points for claims that are factually wrong about Next.js behavior.\",\"estimatedMinutes\":15,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-007\"]}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":257,"output":1024,"cacheRead":642,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00054412,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429261849},"583d91e8813899424e8930b655edfac9b8cf0db9d15076a1c478df3613f6133d":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01PdafSoyBKNncMpJVwik2xT","name":"cite_evidence","input":{"reason":"Nora Sharma's profile has verbatim project sentences for both must-haves: a grounded assistant shipped end to end with a graded held-out set gating releases, and rubric-graded outputs with regression tracking.","candidate_id":"c020","citations":[{"requirement_id":"m1","quote":"Shipped grounded chat assistants end to end: retrieval over internal docs, tool calls behind human approval, and a graded held-out set that gates every release."},{"requirement_id":"m2","quote":"Graded model outputs with rubrics and a held-out set, and tracked regressions per release."},{"requirement_id":"n3","quote":"Shipped tool-calling workflows with human approval before anything irreversible."}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":22346,"output":327,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0024303,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429262071},"a490846185d33709a9894b41f4d156a67fc77f31449d2da17b57eff4c0c0922b":{"response":{"text":"Four candidates are fully evidenced, one short of the target of five. I'll run one more hybrid search with different concrete terms to look for a fifth.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_015D7RFdyuci4F1sTxeNorH3","name":"search_candidates","input":{"reason":"Four fully evidenced candidates, target is five; trying a new hybrid query with different concrete llm-app terms (eval harness, LLM-as-judge, guardrails) to find a fifth profile.","query":"LLM-as-judge eval harness, regression suite for prompts, RAG assistant in production, guardrails, tool-calling agents","mode":"hybrid","k":15}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":24528,"output":337,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0026535,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429264143},"0efbdfb55254192c1f3656ca9b5aab408f38c2482bac6692fc3b96df83ef2cfa":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01Cx65VvQuDRfuT5CQsXryoJ","name":"finalize_shortlist","input":{"reason":"All five HR-approved candidates have verbatim evidence for the three must-haves and graded assessments. Final score is 0.6 semantic plus 0.4 assessment. The fairness audit shows identity had little effect (rho 0.8, top-5 overlap 100%), so the blind ranking is not official.","ranked_ids":["c049","c156","c066","c002","c122"],"weights":{"semantic":0.6,"assessment":0.4},"official_blind":false,"summary":"Shortlist for Senior Backend Engineer, Retrieval Platform, ranked by 0.6 semantic + 0.4 assessment. Tool calls used: 21 of 25. Reformulations: 0. Dropped: none; all five were HR-approved. Final scores: c049 0.87 (sem 0.78, grade 1.00); c156 0.85 (sem 0.75, grade 1.00; code 5/5 in sandbox); c066 0.85 (sem 0.75, grade 1.00); c002 0.74 (sem 0.73, grade 0.75); c122 0.72 (sem 0.74, grade 0.69). Caveats: c049, c066 and c002 have no coding answer, so their grades rest on the rubric alone. c122 and c002 have contradicted claims in grading (c122 pgvector 0.4, c002 FastAPI 0.5), and c122's Postgres evidence came from a strengths-list line, so both need verification before any offer. Fairness audit: rho 0.8, top-5 overlap 100%, no moves of 3 or more places. Gender, age, college and marital-status criteria in the JD were unlawful and were excluded from every judgement."}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":71191,"output":1363,"cacheRead":2109,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.007821690000000001,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429265074},"14f4363bc3c66c253419bf95bf714b31dd916968394d97ad4c3a0c2515c9240b":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"skill-pyspark-001\",\"skill\":\"PySpark\",\"assertion\":\"Rebuilt nightly PySpark jobs and cut the batch window from 6 hours to 50 minutes.\",\"evidence\":\"Rebuilt nightly PySpark jobs on Databricks and cut the batch window from 6 hours to 50 minutes.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"skill-databricks-001\",\"skill\":\"Databricks\",\"assertion\":\"Rebuilt nightly batch jobs on Databricks.\",\"evidence\":\"Rebuilt nightly PySpark jobs on Databricks and cut the batch window from 6 hours to 50 minutes.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"skill-dbt-001\",\"skill\":\"dbt\",\"assertion\":\"Built and tested dbt models, including tests on every mart in a Snowflake warehouse.\",\"evidence\":\"Modelled a Snowflake warehouse with dbt, with tests on every mart.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"skill-dbt-002\",\"skill\":\"dbt\",\"assertion\":\"Tested dbt models that reconcile the finance and product marts.\",\"evidence\":\"Wrote the window-function queries and tested dbt models that reconcile the finance and product marts.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"skill-snowflake-001\",\"skill\":\"Snowflake\",\"assertion\":\"Modelled a Snowflake warehouse.\",\"evidence\":\"Modelled a Snowflake warehouse with dbt, with tests on every mart.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"skill-bigquery-001\",\"skill\":\"BigQuery\",\"assertion\":\"Landed clickstream events in BigQuery within a minute using Kafka consumers.\",\"evidence\":\"Wrote Kafka consumers that land clickstream events in BigQuery within a minute.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"skill-kafka-001\",\"skill\":\"Kafka\",\"assertion\":\"Wrote Kafka consumers that land clickstream events.\",\"evidence\":\"Wrote Kafka consumers that land clickstream events in BigQuery within a minute.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"skill-window-functions-001\",\"skill\":\"Window functions\",\"assertion\":\"Wrote window-function queries.\",\"evidence\":\"Wrote the window-function queries and tested dbt models that reconcile the finance and product marts.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"skill-dag-orchestration-001\",\"skill\":\"DAG orchestration\",\"assertion\":\"Lists DAG orchestration as a strength.\",\"evidence\":\"DAG orchestration\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"skill-experience-001\",\"skill\":\"Years of experience\",\"assertion\":\"Reports 7 years of experience.\",\"years\":7,\"evidence\":\"has 7 years of experience\",\"verified\":false}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":742,"output":1446,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0007972000000000001,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429265463},"3cd705112df66c4fbf32a697f5cd149042e1c97529d87e486474eece301b3da7":{"response":{"text":"Four candidates are fully evidenced, and the remaining llm-apps profiles are the keyword-only ones memory says to drop. I'll propose these four to HR and note the shortfall against the target of five.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01YbQzZa6FbnQmp7xgZyr3tY","name":"request_hr_approval","input":{"reason":"Four candidates have verbatim evidence for both must-haves (LLM applications, evaluation). The pool has no further fully evidenced llm-apps profiles; the remaining ones are keyword-only and were previously dropped.","candidate_ids":["c162","c100","c077","c020"],"rationale":"Shortlist of 4 against a target of 5. Kunal Joshi (c162): grounded assistant with citation checks, held-out set and regression tracking; tool-calling with human approval for agents. Ravi Bose (c100): grounded assistant shipped end to end with a graded held-out set gating releases; tool-calling with human approval. Ivan Petrov (c077): grounded assistant over internal docs; held-out set gating releases; tool-calling with human approval. Nora Sharma (c020): grounded assistant shipped end to end with a held-out set; rubric-graded outputs with regression tracking. RAG is not verified for Ravi Bose after a rejected citation. Prompt engineering and agents are only partially evidenced, and the assessment step will probe the gaps. Pool exhausted after 11 tool calls, with no reformulation beyond the hybrid query."}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":28231,"output":682,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0031963,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429267785},"fe65a4ff1b2157520b8ab361e23237e66523d7c6b3769d62b302d8eb28f55e03":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"q1\",\"type\":\"mcq\",\"prompt\":\"In the Next.js App Router, which file defines the UI that renders for a route segment such as /checkout?\",\"skills\":[\"Next.js\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"answerKey\":\"page.js\",\"options\":[\"page.js\",\"index.html\",\"route.config.js\",\"main.tsx\"],\"estimatedMinutes\":3,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-001\"]},{\"id\":\"q2\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"A checkout modal traps keyboard focus so that Tab never leaves the dialog. Describe how you would diagnose and fix this so keyboard users can always reach and exit the dialog, and name the WCAG 2.1 success criteria you would check.\",\"skills\":[\"WCAG accessibility auditing\"],\"weight\":2,\"required\":true,\"rubric\":\"Full credit (2): Identifies the cause (focus is not released, or Tab/Escape handling is missing or incorrectly intercepted) and proposes a concrete fix, such as a focus trap that cycles only while open, Escape to close, returning focus to the trigger on close, and a visible focus indicator. Names at least two relevant criteria, such as 2.1.1 Keyboard, 2.1.2 No Keyboard Trap, 2.4.3 Focus Order, or 2.4.7 Focus Visible. Partial credit (1): Correct general approach but missing one element, such as criteria, focus return, or Escape handling. No credit (0): Describes a fix that would still trap focus or does not address keyboard operation.\",\"estimatedMinutes\":10,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-005\"]},{\"id\":\"q3\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"In a Next.js App Router project, explain the difference between Server Components and Client Components, and describe when you would add the \\\"use client\\\" directive to a component.\",\"skills\":[\"Next.js\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"rubric\":\"Full credit (2): States that Server Components render on the server by default and can access server-only resources without shipping their JavaScript to the browser, while Client Components are marked with \\\"use client\\\" and are needed for interactivity such as state, effects, or browser event handlers. Partial credit (1): Gets one side right or describes the directive without the reason it is needed. No credit (0): Confuses the two or misstates when the directive applies.\",\"estimatedMinutes\":8,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-001\"]}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":283,"output":1301,"cacheRead":642,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0006852200000000001,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429268261},"92d33e63687b5cd6b601883810559e14db2a3c14e9f7559970ddccb8bc891674":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"q1-databricks-nightly-rerun\",\"type\":\"mcq\",\"prompt\":\"You are rebuilding a nightly batch job on Databricks. The job must be safe to rerun after a partial failure without duplicating rows. Which approach best meets this requirement?\",\"skills\":[\"Databricks\"],\"claimIds\":[\"skill-databricks-001\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"options\":[\"Append each run's output to a Delta table with no key or deduplication step\",\"Use a Delta Lake MERGE keyed on a business identifier, with the batch window tracked so reruns are idempotent\",\"Overwrite the entire target table with a plain CSV export on every run\",\"Write output to a new timestamped folder and let downstream consumers pick the latest one\"],\"answerKey\":\"Use a Delta Lake MERGE keyed on a business identifier, with the batch window tracked so reruns are idempotent\",\"estimatedMinutes\":5},{\"id\":\"q2-dbt-mart-tests\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"In a dbt project on Snowflake, a claim says every mart model has tests. Explain what you would check to verify that claim, and name at least two kinds of tests you would expect to see on a mart and why.\",\"skills\":[\"dbt\"],\"claimIds\":[\"skill-dbt-001\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"rubric\":\"Score 0-4. 1 point: mentions inspecting schema.yml or dbt test results for each mart model, not just a sample. 1 point: names at least two appropriate test types (for example unique, not_null, relationships, accepted_values, or custom singular/generic tests). 1 point: explains why tests matter for marts (business-facing metrics, catching bad joins or duplicates). 1 point: mentions running dbt test or reviewing CI/run results against the Snowflake warehouse to confirm tests actually execute. Deduct the point for any answer that only says 'tests exist' without a verification method.\",\"estimatedMinutes\":10},{\"id\":\"q3-dbt-untested-marts\",\"type\":\"code\",\"prompt\":\"Write a JavaScript function solve(input) where input is a string of comma-separated entries in the form name:testCount (for example 'orders:2,customers:0'). Return a string containing the names of marts with testCount equal to 0, in input order, joined by commas. If none, return an empty string.\",\"skills\":[\"dbt\"],\"claimIds\":[\"skill-dbt-001\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"answerKey\":\"function solve(input) { if (!input) return ''; return input.split(',').filter(e => Number(e.split(':')[1]) === 0).map(e => e.split(':')[0]).join(','); }\",\"tests\":[{\"input\":\"orders:2,customers:0\",\"expected\":\"customers\"},{\"input\":\"a:1,b:1\",\"expected\":\"\"},{\"input\":\"x:0,y:0\",\"expected\":\"x,y\"},{\"input\":\"fact_sales:3,dim_date:0,dim_store:5\",\"expected\":\"dim_date\"}],\"estimatedMinutes\":15}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":272,"output":1724,"cacheRead":642,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00089562,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429274450},"d0deeb194cc77685fb2c1ba00dcf3ce9bfb47a4ef2e38b50b5720a3c113a5266":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"q-001\",\"type\":\"mcq\",\"prompt\":\"In the Next.js App Router, which component type runs on the server by default and can fetch data without shipping its component code to the browser?\",\"skills\":[\"Next.js\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":true,\"options\":[\"Server Component\",\"Client Component\",\"Edge Middleware\",\"API Route Handler\"],\"answerKey\":\"Server Component\",\"claimIds\":[\"claim-001\"],\"estimatedMinutes\":5},{\"id\":\"q-002\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"You are rebuilding a reusable Button component in TypeScript under strict mode and must not use `any`. Describe how you would type its props so that it accepts standard button attributes, a `variant` prop limited to 'primary' | 'secondary', and an optional `onClick` handler. Explain how you avoid `any` and how you would handle an unknown extra prop.\",\"skills\":[\"TypeScript\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":true,\"rubric\":\"Full credit (3): Uses an interface or type extending React.ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement> (or equivalent) with variant typed as a string-literal union; explains that no 'any' is needed and that unknown props are rejected by the compiler or stripped via rest destructuring; mentions strict null handling for optional onClick. Partial (1-2): Correct props shape but misses one of the no-any, literal union, or extra-prop explanations. No credit (0): Relies on 'any', 'as any', or untyped props, or describes no concrete typing approach.\",\"claimIds\":[\"claim-004\"],\"estimatedMinutes\":10},{\"id\":\"q-003\",\"type\":\"code\",\"prompt\":\"Implement solve(input) in JavaScript. The input is a JSON string representing an array of objects like {\\\"name\\\": string, \\\"score\\\": number}. Return the name of the object with the highest score. If there is a tie, return the name that appears first in the array. Return an empty string for an empty array.\",\"skills\":[\"TypeScript\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"answerKey\":\"function solve(input) { const arr = JSON.parse(input); if (arr.length === 0) return ''; let best = arr[0]; for (const item of arr) { if (item.score > best.score) best = item; } return best.name; }\",\"tests\":[{\"input\":\"[{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Ana\\\",\\\"score\\\":3},{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Ben\\\",\\\"score\\\":7},{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Cy\\\",\\\"score\\\":5}]\",\"expected\":\"Ben\"},{\"input\":\"[{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Ana\\\",\\\"score\\\":4},{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Ben\\\",\\\"score\\\":4}]\",\"expected\":\"Ana\"},{\"input\":\"[]\",\"expected\":\"\"}],\"claimIds\":[\"claim-004\"],\"estimatedMinutes\":10}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":256,"output":1565,"cacheRead":642,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00081452,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429276592},"4af5cdd08521408ee698299175a39105ea1dbf617602bc03f0ab1c48fa0ef4f5":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"claim-001\",\"skill\":\"PySpark\",\"assertion\":\"Focuses on building PySpark jobs.\",\"evidence\":\"Builder focused on pyspark and databricks jobs.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-002\",\"skill\":\"Databricks\",\"assertion\":\"Rebuilt nightly PySpark jobs on Databricks, cutting the batch window from 6 hours to 50 minutes.\",\"evidence\":\"Rebuilt nightly PySpark jobs on Databricks and cut the batch window from 6 hours to 50 minutes.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-003\",\"skill\":\"Snowflake\",\"assertion\":\"Modelled a Snowflake warehouse.\",\"evidence\":\"Modelled a Snowflake warehouse with dbt, with tests on every mart.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-004\",\"skill\":\"BigQuery modelling\",\"assertion\":\"Describes BigQuery modelling as a strength.\",\"evidence\":\"Snowflake and BigQuery modelling\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-005\",\"skill\":\"dbt\",\"assertion\":\"Uses dbt for modelling, with tests on every mart.\",\"evidence\":\"Modelled a Snowflake warehouse with dbt, with tests on every mart.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-006\",\"skill\":\"dbt\",\"assertion\":\"Tested dbt models that reconcile the finance and product marts.\",\"evidence\":\"tested dbt models that reconcile the finance and product marts.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-007\",\"skill\":\"DAG orchestration\",\"assertion\":\"Owned DAG orchestration for 200+ daily tasks with retries and SLA alerts.\",\"evidence\":\"Owned DAG orchestration for 200+ daily tasks with retries and SLA alerts.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-008\",\"skill\":\"SQL window functions\",\"assertion\":\"Wrote window-function queries.\",\"evidence\":\"Wrote the window-function queries\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-009\",\"skill\":\"Data engineering experience\",\"assertion\":\"Has 7 years of experience.\",\"evidence\":\"has 7 years of experience\",\"years\":7,\"verified\":false}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":581,"output":1311,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0007136,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429279419},"6acf47c3674b1522026bf06e6e74a7f744d9260f4bc71f085f95752d7931e368":{"response":{"text":"{\"label\":\"no\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"rationale\":\"The requirement 'search for people who are good at ML' names a job-relevant skill and references no protected characteristic (gender, age, religion, caste, marital/family status, disability, national origin) and does not use college name as a proxy for pedigree. It is not discriminatory on its face.\"}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":200,"output":112,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.000076,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429282081},"be30d8bff5b78fd83f0f055712ad39312dcc9b62ff7b32533fc3c9bf6856b26e":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"claim-001\",\"skill\":\"Next.js\",\"assertion\":\"Works with Next.js to build UIs\",\"years\":0,\"evidence\":\"Practitioner working on next.js and preact uis.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-002\",\"skill\":\"Preact\",\"assertion\":\"Works with Preact to build UIs\",\"years\":0,\"evidence\":\"Practitioner working on next.js and preact uis.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-003\",\"skill\":\"Frontend development\",\"assertion\":\"Has 3 years of experience\",\"years\":3,\"evidence\":\"has 3 years of experience\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-004\",\"skill\":\"Storybook\",\"assertion\":\"Builds components driven by Storybook and writes Storybook docs for every component\",\"evidence\":\"with Storybook docs for every piece\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-005\",\"skill\":\"Design systems\",\"assertion\":\"Shipped a shared UI kit with theme tokens adopted by six product squads\",\"evidence\":\"Shipped a shared UI kit with theme tokens adopted by six product squads\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-006\",\"skill\":\"Web performance optimization\",\"assertion\":\"Brought Largest Contentful Paint under 1.5s on the marketing site using code-splitting and image work\",\"evidence\":\"Brought Largest Contentful Paint under 1.5s on the marketing site by code-splitting and image work.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-007\",\"skill\":\"TypeScript\",\"assertion\":\"Rebuilt the component kit under strict static typing with zero any usage\",\"evidence\":\"Rebuilt the component kit under strict static typing (tsc --strict, zero any)\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-008\",\"skill\":\"Web accessibility (WCAG)\",\"assertion\":\"Ran WCAG 2.1 audits and fixed focus traps across the checkout flow\",\"evidence\":\"Ran WCAG 2.1 audits with screen readers and fixed focus traps across the checkout flow.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-009\",\"skill\":\"Screen-reader testing\",\"assertion\":\"Tests interfaces with screen readers\",\"evidence\":\"Ran WCAG 2.1 audits with screen readers and fixed focus traps across the checkout flow.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"claim-010\",\"skill\":\"ARIA and keyboard accessibility\",\"assertion\":\"Implemented ARIA roles, keyboard navigation and screen-reader labels on every control in the component kit\",\"evidence\":\"with ARIA roles, keyboard navigation and screen-reader labels on every control.\",\"verified\":false}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":622,"output":1498,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0008112000000000001,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429282548},"fc7be373a259d9d77fc70214351d98f4217de48242e4b0fb0f72f4e4a15c210c":{"response":{"text":"{\"title\":\"Machine Learning Specialist\",\"seniority\":\"unspecified\",\"must_have\":[\"Machine learning fundamentals\",\"Proficiency in Python\",\"Model training and evaluation\"],\"nice_to_have\":[\"Deep learning frameworks\",\"Data preprocessing experience\",\"Production model deployment\"]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":168,"output":101,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0000673,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429283218},"bc8cdc1bfeee0a4fa46d8ae263e3a14aa94a6e83ea1a7a5b99589b55bc93076a":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01EFidnADuxfDYLUtHxQAJTR","name":"recall_memory","input":{"reason":"Starting session; checking for saved HR notes on this role before sourcing.","role":"custom"}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":382,"output":94,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00011740000000000001,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793429284211},"b13f69d075ea8bc29440185fc23d04fba9e278ee5a6e12c89323f6cb7049b94c":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"q-001\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"Describe how you would structure a PySpark batch job that reads a large partitioned dataset, applies transformations, and writes output. Explain how you would check and improve its performance, including at least one specific technique (for example, partitioning, caching, broadcast joins, or avoiding shuffles).\",\"skills\":[\"PySpark\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"rubric\":\"Score out of 4. 1 point: correct overall flow (read, transform, write). 1 point: explains how to find bottlenecks (Spark UI, stage/shuffle metrics, skew). 1 point: names at least one concrete optimization and why it helps. 1 point: explains a trade-off or failure mode (e.g., broadcast size limits, too many small files, over-caching memory pressure). Award 0 for vague answers with no Spark-specific detail.\",\"estimatedMinutes\":10,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-001\"]},{\"id\":\"q-002\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"A nightly batch pipeline runs on Databricks and takes 6 hours. Describe the steps you would take to reduce its runtime substantially, and how you would verify that the improvement is real and that the output is unchanged. Do not assume any specific prior results.\",\"skills\":[\"Databricks\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"rubric\":\"Score out of 4. 1 point: profiles first (Spark UI or Databricks job/cluster metrics) before changing code. 1 point: proposes Databricks-relevant changes (cluster sizing or autoscaling, Photon or runtime choice, Delta table optimization such as OPTIMIZE/ZORDER or file compaction, incremental processing). 1 point: describes validation of output equivalence (row counts, checksums, reconciliation). 1 point: describes measuring runtime before and after with a fair comparison (same data volume, repeated runs). Award 0 for generic answers that name no Databricks-specific mechanism.\",\"estimatedMinutes\":12,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-002\"]},{\"id\":\"q-003\",\"type\":\"mcq\",\"prompt\":\"In PySpark, which operation is generally preferred to reduce the number of partitions without triggering a full shuffle?\",\"skills\":[\"PySpark\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"options\":[\"repartition(n)\",\"coalesce(n)\",\"collect()\",\"broadcast()\"],\"answerKey\":\"coalesce(n)\",\"e