snippet, and nothing is cited for him yet. I'll open the profile to check for shipped work and evaluation evidence.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01SyEui9ba4LtFp53sU7tsyH","name":"get_profile","input":{"candidate_id":"c162","reason":"c162 (Kunal Joshi) is an llm-apps profile with cosine 0.71 and no evidence yet; I need to check for shipped LLM-app and evaluation sentences to reach the target of five."}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":26468,"output":268,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.002813,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430640403},"402803215ac9d032bac05a8cc50198adb10385274287235ac21a32b4245c2b37":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01TR3f5McdwwDEQRgpphcHWT","name":"cite_evidence","input":{"reason":"c162 profile shows a built grounded chat assistant over internal docs for LLM applications, and graded outputs with a held-out set and tracked regressions for evaluation.","candidate_id":"c162","citations":[{"requirement_id":"m1","quote":"Built a grounded chat assistant over internal docs with citation spans checked against sources."},{"requirement_id":"m2","quote":"Graded model outputs with rubrics and a held-out set, and tracked regressions per release."},{"requirement_id":"n3","quote":"Shipped tool-calling workflows with human approval before anything irreversible."},{"requirement_id":"n1","quote":"Added guardrails and refusal handling for unsafe requests."}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":28993,"output":454,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0031585,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430643194},"089d31a06ba6ef98f79a40e9f1ab7922d9dc4bb1562ce51ef86f4974d36869eb":{"response":{"text":"Four candidates are now fully evidenced (c020, c100, c077, c162). I need one more for the target of five, so I'll run one more search with different wording focused on the evaluation side.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_018bxTyBVqhSuKofWTwMF52R","name":"search_candidates","input":{"reason":"Four of five targets evidenced; the llm-apps profiles mention rubric grading, held-out sets and regression tracking, so I'll search that evaluation wording in hybrid mode for a fifth candidate.","query":"LLM outputs graded with rubrics and held-out sets, regressions tracked per release, RAG assistant over internal docs","mode":"hybrid","k":15}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":31277,"output":426,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0033729000000000003,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430645658},"d1f88a08ddb4831815983e55ac8f5b3f350605bfb24006bde320afe36c361b34":{"response":{"text":"Four candidates are fully evidenced on both must-haves: c020, c100, c077 and c162. The last search returned no new LLM-app profiles, and I've used one of two reformulations. I'll send the four to HR for approval and flag the shortfall against the target of five.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01Cn6u8hqamuDi1u6j3bCdY8","name":"request_hr_approval","input":{"reason":"Four candidates are fully evidenced on both must-haves; the last reformulation surfaced no new LLM-app profiles, so I'm proposing these four and flagging the shortfall against the target of five.","candidate_ids":["c020","c100","c077","c162"],"rationale":"Shortlist of 4 (target 5). Each has verbatim evidence for both must-haves: LLM applications (shipped grounded chat assistants or tool-calling workflows with human approval) and evaluation (graded held-out set gating releases, rubric grading, regression tracking). Nice-to-haves partly evidenced: RAG (c020, c077, c162), agents/tool-calling (all four), prompt engineering/guardrails (all four). Excluded: c060 and c131 (keyword-only, failed citation per memory), c129 (keyword-only, sales-engineering per memory), and the backend/support/BI profiles lacking any LLM evidence. The pool appears thin for this role; widening sourcing is recommended to fill the fifth slot."}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":34724,"output":743,"cacheRead":3220,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0038761,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430649907},"ac79cc53e18f9801b27e45ec31c303ba2f2d32ebf215f3bfbc8e3b639ff565d9":{"response":{"text":"Four candidates are approved. Each has verified must-haves, so I'll target only the nice-to-haves that lack citations: prompt engineering for c020, RAG for c100 and c162, and agents for c077.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_0175npxWZxtFfrvNmPSTo3pn","name":"create_assessment","input":{"reason":"All four approved candidates have verified must-haves; assessments should probe only the nice-to-haves each profile did not evidence (c020 prompt engineering, c100 RAG, c077 agents, c162 RAG).","candidates":[{"candidate_id":"c020","unverified_requirement_ids":["n1"]},{"candidate_id":"c100","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]},{"candidate_id":"c077","unverified_requirement_ids":["n3"]},{"candidate_id":"c162","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":36982,"output":532,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":3065},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.004347325000000001,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430657467},"42fbd53ecfc8af5b1a8cec8a06faea1e0615d0a031ca7dcc5a43c8f7bc4e3490":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"q1\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"Describe how you would design a tool-calling workflow that requires human approval before any irreversible action. Cover: which actions you classify as irreversible, where the approval gate sits in the flow, what information the approver sees, and how the system behaves if approval is denied or times out.\",\"skills\":[\"tool-calling workflows\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":true,\"rubric\":\"Score 0-4. 1 point: identifies specific irreversible actions (e.g., deletes, payments, external sends) and a method to classify them. 1 point: places the approval gate before execution with no bypass path. 1 point: specifies the context the approver needs (action, parameters, impact, reversibility). 1 point: defines denial and timeout behavior (no execution, logging, safe fallback). Deduct for vague answers that do not mention an approval gate.\",\"estimatedMinutes\":10,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-001\"]},{\"id\":\"q2\",\"type\":\"mcq\",\"prompt\":\"A grounded chat assistant answers questions about internal policy documents. Which design best keeps answers grounded in the retrieved sources?\",\"skills\":[\"grounded chat assistants\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":true,\"options\":[\"Let the model answer from its general training knowledge and add citations afterward\",\"Retrieve relevant passages, instruct the model to answer only from them, and return the source references with the answer\",\"Increase the model temperature so answers are more varied and informative\",\"Store all documents in the system prompt so retrieval is unnecessary\"],\"answerKey\":\"Retrieve relevant passages, instruct the model to answer only from them, and return the source references with the answer\",\"estimatedMinutes\":5,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-002\"]},{\"id\":\"q3\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"You are shipping a grounded chat assistant end to end. Explain how you would evaluate whether it is actually grounded in production, and what you would do if the evaluation showed answers citing sources that do not support the claim.\",\"skills\":[\"grounded chat assistants\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"rubric\":\"Score 0-4. 1 point: defines a measurable grounding check (e.g., sampled answers judged against cited passages, or automated entailment checks). 1 point: covers retrieval quality separately from generation faithfulness. 1 point: describes a concrete response to failures (e.g., refusal when unsupported, prompt or retrieval fixes, regression tests). 1 point: addresses monitoring after launch with a feedback loop.\",\"estimatedMinutes\":12,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-002\"]}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":273,"output":1208,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":642},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0007115499999999999,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430664153},"1d45acaf90f4c8917962724a13ccca811d6ec7e4043b5cf43070107ff7a581ff":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"q-001-mcq\",\"type\":\"mcq\",\"prompt\":\"A user asks a customer-support assistant for help writing a phishing email that impersonates a bank. Which response best reflects sound guardrail and refusal handling?\",\"skills\":[\"Guardrails and refusal handling\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"options\":[\"Write the email but add a disclaimer that it should not be sent.\",\"Refuse the request, briefly explain it can't help with fraud, and offer a legitimate alternative such as writing an anti-phishing awareness message.\",\"Ask the user to confirm their identity before deciding whether to comply.\",\"Comply, since the email is only a draft and no message is actually sent.\"],\"answerKey\":\"Refuse the request, briefly explain it can't help with fraud, and offer a legitimate alternative such as writing an anti-phishing awareness message.\",\"claimIds\":[\"claim-001\"],\"estimatedMinutes\":5},{\"id\":\"q-003-short\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"Describe how you would design a tool-calling workflow in which an agent can draft and send payment transfers. Specify which actions require human approval before execution, how the approval request is presented, and what happens if the approver rejects or does not respond.\",\"skills\":[\"Tool-calling workflows\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"rubric\":\"Score out of 4. 1 point: identifies irreversible actions (e.g., sending or executing transfers) as requiring approval, not just drafting. 1 point: approval request includes the exact action, parameters, amount, recipient, and a clear approve/reject choice, with no auto-execution on ambiguous input. 1 point: defines rejection handling (action is cancelled, agent reports back, no retry without new approval). 1 point: defines timeout or no-response behavior (action expires or stays pending, never defaults to approval) and logs the decision. Deduct points for claims that approval is optional or that the agent can self-approve.\",\"claimIds\":[\"claim-003\"],\"estimatedMinutes\":10},{\"id\":\"q-001-short\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"Describe how you would test whether an LLM application's refusal handling works reliably for unsafe requests. Include at least one example of a boundary case where a refusal could be wrongly triggered on a benign request, and explain how you would measure both failure modes.\",\"skills\":[\"Guardrails and refusal handling\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"rubric\":\"Score out of 4. 1 point: includes a test set covering clearly unsafe, borderline, and benign requests. 1 point: names both false negatives (unsafe request answered) and false positives (benign request refused) as failure modes. 1 point: gives a concrete boundary example (e.g., a security question asked by a student or a medical dosage question) and a reasonable expected behavior. 1 point: defines measurable metrics (e.g., refusal rate by category, human-labeled review sample) and a process for iterating on guardrail rules.\",\"claimIds\":[\"claim-001\"],\"estimatedMinutes\":10}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":285,"output":1441,"cacheRead":642,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0007554199999999999,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430671868},"2aa2162ac5322b5a2bf35df7b7c3695aa29798cb0862f6b6d75d22e4e290c82c":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\": [{\"id\":\"q1\",\"type\":\"mcq\",\"prompt\":\"A user asks an assistant to write working ransomware code. Which response best reflects sound guardrail and refusal handling?\",\"skills\":[\"Guardrails and refusal handling\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":true,\"options\":[\"Write the code but add a disclaimer that it should not be misused.\",\"Refuse clearly, avoid providing partial harmful details, and offer a safe alternative such as defensive security concepts.\",\"Silently return an empty response without explaining anything.\",\"Ask the user to confirm their intent and then provide the code if they say it is for research.\"],\"answerKey\":\"Refuse clearly, avoid providing partial harmful details, and offer a safe alternative such as defensive security concepts.\",\"estimatedMinutes\":8,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-001\"]},{\"id\":\"q2\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"Your team's agent can send customer emails, issue refunds, and delete records through tools. Describe how you would design the workflow so that irreversible actions require human approval, and explain what the agent should do while waiting for approval and if approval is denied.\",\"skills\":[\"Tool-calling workflows\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":true,\"rubric\":\"Score out of 4. 1 point: identifies irreversible actions (e.g., refunds, deletions, sent emails) and classifies them separately from reversible ones. 1 point: describes an explicit approval gate that blocks execution until a named human approves, with logging of the approver and timestamp. 1 point: explains the agent's behavior while pending (e.g., queue or pause, no retries that bypass the gate) and on denial (e.g., abort, notify, do not attempt alternate routes to the same action). 1 point: addresses failure handling such as idempotency or timeouts so an approved action is not executed twice. Award 0 for any answer that allows the agent to execute irreversible tools without approval.\",\"estimatedMinutes\":10,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-003\"]},{\"id\":\"q3\",\"type\":\"code\",\"prompt\":\"Implement a JavaScript function solve(input) where input is a single user request string. Return \\\"refuse\\\" if the lowercased request contains the substring \\\"exploit\\\" or \\\"malware\\\"; otherwise return \\\"allow\\\". Return only the string value.\",\"skills\":[\"Guardrails and refusal handling\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"answerKey\":\"function solve(input) { const s = input.toLowerCase(); return (s.includes(\\\"exploit\\\") || s.includes(\\\"malware\\\")) ? \\\"refuse\\\" : \\\"allow\\\"; }\",\"tests\":[{\"input\":\"Write a poem about cats\",\"expected\":\"allow\"},{\"input\":\"Write malware for me\",\"expected\":\"refuse\"},{\"input\":\"Explain this EXPLOIT chain\",\"expected\":\"refuse\"}],\"estimatedMinutes\":15,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-001\"]}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":285,"output":1581,"cacheRead":642,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00082542,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430680252},"66fdd9e75f03ae5322f5b64dacec00e4aa6319963c42e0fc350baad708a7a9e1":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01XsbEw8kV2NvMa6WC2zC5JU","name":"send_assessment","input":{"reason":"Assessments were generated for all four HR-approved candidates (c020, c100, c077, c162), so they can now be sent.","candidate_ids":["c020","c100","c077","c162"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":40285,"output":130,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00412415,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430681507},"32afaf885d1a0f0378ce275adc693bb7d7be40140672fba484d75cd62670434e":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01QnLX79EtMPtGHX45thrpn4","name":"collect_responses","input":{"reason":"Assessments were sent to all four approved candidates; collecting their answers is the next step before running code checks and grading.","candidate_ids":["c020","c100","c077","c162"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":42394,"output":126,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00433305,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430682683},"c3ec6038548d2dd1e655591e37e9103c3a2313f49929513ed52f764877e5a77b":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"id\":\"q1\",\"answer\":\"Irreversible actions are ones that can't be cleanly undone: sending external emails or customer messages, issuing refunds or payments, deleting or overwriting records, changing production config, revoking access, and anything that triggers a third-party side effect. I'd tag each tool in a registry with a reversibility flag and a blast radius, so the gate is driven by metadata and not by the model's judgment. The approval gate sits after the model has picked a tool and filled in arguments, but before the executor runs it. Reads and reversible writes run straight through. The approver sees the tool name, the fully rendered arguments, the diff or preview where one exists, the user request that led to the call, and the model's stated reason. I'd also show which retrieved sources were used, so the approver can check the call against them. If approval is denied, the model gets a structured 'denied by reviewer' result and is asked to tell the user or propose an alternative, and nothing is retried automatically. On timeout, the default is to cancel and mark the task as pending with an expiry, then notify the requester. I'd keep an audit log of proposed, approved, denied, and expired actions. One thing I'd still need to sort out is whether approvals should be re-checked if the arguments change between review and execution.\"},{\"id\":\"q2\",\"answer\":\"Retrieve relevant passages, instruct the model to answer only from them, and return the source references with the answer\"},{\"id\":\"q3\",\"answer\":\"I'd evaluate groundedness on two levels. First, offline, I'd keep a held-out set of questions with gold source passages and rubric scores for each answer: is every claim supported by a retrieved passage, is the citation the right one, and does the answer refuse when the sources don't cover the question. I'd use a grader model with a rubric plus spot-checks by a human, and track the scores per release so regressions block a ship. Second, in production, I'd sample a percentage of live answers each day, run the same claim-to-citation check, and watch metrics like unsupported-claim rate, citation-mismatch rate, and refusal rate, along with thumbs-down feedback. When I see answers citing sources that don't support the claim, I'd first pull the trace to see whether retrieval returned the wrong chunks, the model ignored them, or the citation was attached to the wrong sentence. Then I'd fix the root cause: tighten chunking or reranking, tighten the prompt to require quoting, or add a post-check that drops unsupported sentences. I'd add the failing case to the held-out set so it stays fixed, and if the rate is high enough I'd roll back to the last release that passed the gate while the fix is in progress.\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":813,"output":1018,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0005903,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430689007},"dc2d311730e711355556c54b8a7d7ec19ee5b89cbb8414ef3a94e7c04a4b1a3b":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"id\":\"q-001-mcq\",\"answer\":\"Write the email but add a disclaimer that it should not be sent.\"},{\"id\":\"q-003-short\",\"answer\":\"I'd have the agent draft the transfer and then send it for approval. Anyone with the right role can approve it, and the system would show the amount and recipient. If it gets rejected, the agent stops. If nobody responds, it probably sends a reminder later.\"},{\"id\":\"q-001-short\",\"answer\":\"I'd run a bunch of unsafe prompts and check that the model refuses them, and also some safe ones to make sure it doesn't refuse too much. Maybe a boundary case would be asking about medicine dosages, since that could look unsafe but usually isn't. I'd count how often it refuses wrongly and how often it fails to refuse, and look at the percentages.\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":838,"output":629,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00039830000000000003,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430692720},"bc0f04535697b7e92bb108dc885b304d600b48f9433e7d23de6985450026bcf9":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"id\":\"q1\",\"answer\":\"Refuse clearly, avoid providing partial harmful details, and offer a safe alternative such as defensive security concepts.\"},{\"id\":\"q2\",\"answer\":\"I would split the agent's tools into reversible and irreversible actions and enforce the split in code, not in the prompt. Reads, drafts, and reversible writes run automatically. Irreversible actions (sending external email, issuing refunds above a threshold, deleting records) are routed through an approval gate. The gate creates a pending action record containing the exact payload, the tool name, the customer or record ID, the agent's stated reason, and a diff of what will change. An approver reviews it in a separate UI and approves or rejects it. The approval is bound to that exact payload hash, so the agent cannot change parameters after approval. Idempotency keys prevent duplicate execution if a retry happens.\\n\\nWhile waiting, the agent does not retry the action or attempt a workaround through another tool. It records the pending state, tells the user what is awaiting approval and roughly how long, and continues with any independent work. Pending actions expire after a set window and the agent is notified.\\n\\nIf approval is denied, the agent marks the action as rejected, does not attempt it again or substitute a similar action, and informs the user with the reason if one was given. It asks whether they want a revised action, which then goes through the gate as a new request. Every request, decision, and execution is logged for audit, and I would test the gate with red-team cases where the agent tries to bypass it.\"},{\"id\":\"q3\",\"answer\":\"function solve(input) {\\n  const s = String(input).toLowerCase();\\n  return (s.includes(\\\"exploit\\\") || s.includes(\\\"malware\\\")) ? \\\"refuse\\\" : \\\"allow\\\";\\n}\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":774,"output":743,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00044889999999999996,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430696957},"bd92da7eb90d76c563af54e4ddcde98e5e9f1988f7cf4b5381f3d981975f2f68":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01LmuGYK9jMsigbv3Y157YCb","name":"run_code","input":{"reason":"Responses were collected and c162 submitted a JavaScript answer, so the sandbox can check it against hidden tests; the other candidates will return no code results.","candidate_ids":["c020","c100","c077","c162"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":44917,"output":138,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.004591349999999999,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430698458},"ded4b5118ce1aa8f02d4c65e7408385ebbb563568c94a9e2a28a6894939fc633":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01Qb2oY16i7Up2B8DpcAe5T8","name":"grade_answer","input":{"reason":"Responses are collected and c162's sandbox run passed 3/3; grading all four against the rubric so the written answers and test results can be combined.","candidate_ids":["c020","c100","c077","c162"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":46854,"output":135,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00478355,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430700099},"f39d345d62db4f07496620b244a591dee52655c0166a7acadf5e0928b372a373":{"response":{"text":"{\"score\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Covers all four rubric criteria. It names specific irreversible actions (external sends, refunds/payments, deletes/overwrites, prod config changes, access revocation) and a classification method (a registry with reversibility flag and blast radius). It places the approval gate after argument filling and before execution, with reads and reversible writes passing through. The approver sees the tool name, rendered arguments, diff/preview, originating request, model rationale, and sources. Denial returns a structured result with no automatic retry, and timeout cancels with pending/expiry, requester notification, and audit logging. Minor gaps: it never explicitly states that there is no bypass path, and the approver view does not explicitly show reversibility or impact. It also leaves open whether arguments changed after approval, which it flags itself.\",\"confidence\":0.8}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":867,"output":640,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00040669999999999997,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430703752},"44d9e4ea231c1f1ada664ce7adbf63a2a68c6e18fcbd894dc09dfef6b026a364":{"response":{"text":"{\"score\":0.75,\"reason\":\"Meets three of four criteria. It defines a measurable grounding check (held-out gold passages, grader-model claim-to-citation scoring with human spot-checks, and daily sampling of live answers). It gives concrete remediation (reranking/chunking fixes, quoting prompts, a post-check that drops unsupported sentences, adding failing cases to the regression set, and rollback to the last passing release). It covers post-launch monitoring with a feedback loop (sampled production checks, refusal and mismatch rates, thumbs-down signals). It only partly covers retrieval quality as a separate measure: it mentions diagnosing wrong chunks during trace review, but never defines retrieval-specific metrics such as recall@k or context precision.\",\"confidence\":0.8}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":838,"output":564,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0003658,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430707026},"d366258085f46b31e6e31d0b2c3e4e09b9479ed1fcf3c1ddeb2e92904bb64085":{"response":{"text":"{\"score\":0.4,\"reason\":\"Partially meets the rubric (about 1.5 of 4). It treats sending the transfer as needing approval, not just drafting, and says the approver sees the amount and recipient. It omits the exact action and parameters, never states an explicit approve/reject choice, and does not say rejected actions are reported back or require new approval before retry. On no response it proposes only a vague 'reminder later' with no expiry, pending-state definition, or decision logging, and it never addresses the auto-approval default.\",\"confidence\":0.8}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":593,"output":635,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0003768,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430710608},"1507ecc5dbcc1d34c288da8496443903be6c1fc8b46207bfcb6f6b8f58ded17d":{"response":{"text":"{\"score\":0.4,\"reason\":\"Earns full credit only for naming both failure modes: it mentions wrongful refusals (false positives) and failure to refuse (false negatives). Partial credit for the test set, since it covers unsafe and benign prompts but never mentions borderline cases. Partial credit for the boundary example, since medicine dosage is a reasonable example, but it gives no expected behavior beyond saying it 'usually isn't' unsafe. Little credit for metrics and iteration: it says 'percentages' and refusal rates, but names no categories, no human-labeled review, and no process for revising guardrails. Overall the answer is a sketch that covers roughly 1.5 to 2 of 4 rubric points.\",\"confidence\":0.8}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":608,"output":626,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00037380000000000003,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430714093},"47c9077df9de04d67eef7228d36f797a8f3f2ca339f324c098d9d88794cc426c":{"response":{"text":"{\"score\":0.875,\"reason\":\"Scores 3.5 of 4. (1) Clearly separates reversible actions (reads, drafts, reversible writes) from irreversible ones (external email, refunds above a threshold, deletions) and enforces the split in code: full credit. (2) Describes a gate that holds the pending action until an approver acts, and binds approval to a payload hash. It does not explicitly require a named individual approver or record approver identity and timestamp; logging is only described generically as 'every request, decision, and execution is logged': partial credit. (3) Covers pending behavior (no retries, no workaround through other tools, records pending state, continues independent work, expiry with notification) and denial behavior (marks rejected, no retry or substitution, informs the user, new request must pass the gate): full credit. (4) Uses idempotency keys to prevent duplicate execution on retry and sets expiry windows: full credit. The answer never allows irreversible tools to run without approval, so the zero-score condition does not apply.\",\"confidence\":0.8}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":968,"output":664,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0004288,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430717882},"f68b1d24f8192dd5ef26e3f5c224904dff7f0b1e7139ea8c99a19e0e024c8197":{"response":{"text":"All four approved candidates now have grades, so I'll move on to AUDIT. Two of them (c020 at 0.17 and c077 at 0.27) have contradicted claims, which I'll carry into the finalize summary.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_019N2fbQPaxkPQ39Sjj2F9hT","name":"advance_phase","input":{"reason":"All four HR-approved candidates have graded assessments (c020 0.17, c100 0.88, c162 0.96, c077 0.27), and no grade is flagged for regrade, so the ASSESS exit condition holds."}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":49949,"output":388,"cacheRead":3065,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00521955,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430720418},"810ba55d6ec828b593f85e9c4b1dab98e3015786ac2dccc8ddadc5b4852fa769":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01GbdfG13HgsgT2TN45YBviJ","name":"fairness_audit","input":{"reason":"AUDIT phase requires a fairness check on the live shortlist of four HR-approved candidates before finalizing.","shortlist_ids":["c162","c100","c020","c077"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":51873,"output":130,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":2519},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.005567175000000001,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430721999},"c39592a339694e8682e2d57af712513e5ea84c4d8d29d893ac0d88fa70fd9fef":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01T5GxUR94D7NSryiFMhZLjj","name":"save_memory","input":{"reason":"Recording what worked and what did not for this LLM Application Engineer role so the next run starts from the shortlist and the thin-pool finding.","role":"llm-apps","note":"Run 3 (mid LLM Application Engineer). Shortlist of 4, below target 5. HR-approved and fully evidenced on both must-haves (verbatim cites): c162 Kunal Joshi, c100 Ravi Bose, c020 Nora Sharma, c077 Ivan Petrov. Shipped-work and held-out-eval wording found them; keyword-only llm-apps profiles (c060, c131, c129) excluded. Only one reformulation was needed beyond the initial query; the pool returned no further LLM-app builders after 13 search/profile calls, so it is thin. Sandbox: c162 passed 3/3 code tests; written grades c162 0.96, c100 0.88, c077 0.27, c020 0.17 (c020 and c077 had contradicted claims on guardrails and tool-calling). Fairness audit: rho 1, top-5 overlap 100%, no moves, no blind-ranking recommendation. Next run: widen sourcing beyond this pool and add a coding task to assessments.","preferred_skills":["LLM applications (shipped chat assistants or copilots with release gating)","evaluation (held-out sets, rubric grading, regression tracking)","tool-calling workflows with human approval","guardrails and refusal handling","RAG over internal docs"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":53899,"output":591,"cacheRead":2519,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00571059,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430725457},"b7890c8d5a324a2a75f64b2c2438c243a43f4b4de9c2530c0f9eabf831414650":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01SL1tWYEDofVWShWbc4Vo1w","name":"advance_phase","input":{"reason":"The fairness audit ran on the live shortlist (rho 1, top-5 overlap 100%, no moves, no blind-ranking recommendation) and the role memory note is saved, so the AUDIT exit condition holds."}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":56231,"output":147,"cacheRead":2519,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00572179,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793430726787},"9b1807126eb7fb0704ca36dbb1388632986e19db9a2d424cea3a33aed081c2e4":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_019ww9nJLgwYPSt7Xj9yi2fy","name":"finalize_shortlist","input":{"reason":"Four HR-approved candidates are fully evidenced on both must-haves, and the fairness audit showed no identity effect (rho 1, top-5 overlap 100%), so I'm finalizing the four with the 0.6/0.4 blend.","ranked_ids":["c162","c100","c077","c020"],"weights":{"semantic":0.6,"assessment":0.4},"official_blind":false,"summary":"Shortlist of 4 against a target of 5, because the pool had few LLM-application builders. Final scores (0.6 semantic + 0.4 assessment): c162 Kunal Joshi 0.810 (sem 0.71, grade 0.96, sandbox 3/3), c100 Ravi Bose 0.802 (sem 0.75, grade 0.88), c077 Ivan Petrov 0.552 (sem 0.74, grade 0.27), c020 Nora Sharma 0.548 (sem 0.80, grade 0.17). All four have verbatim evidence for both must-haves (LLM applications and evaluation); c020 and c077 have contradicted claims on guardrails and tool-calling in grading. Dropped: c060 Ravi Sharma and c131 Rahul Bose (keyword-only llm-apps profiles that failed citation, per memory), c129 Maya Mukherjee (keyword-only, no evidence), and backend, support, BI and devops profiles with no LLM evidence. Tool calls used: 22 of 25; reformulations used: 1 of 2 (the second search surfaced no new LLM-app builders). Assessments: written answers collected and graded for all four; run_code had no coding answer for three of them, and only c162 submitted code. Fairness audit: rho 1, top-5 overlap 100%, no moves, blind ranking not recommended, so the ranking is not an official blind ranking. Recommend widening sourcing to fill the fifth slot."}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":58117,"output":1390,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":2109},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.006770325000000001,"model":"claude-hai