ted profile; check whether it has project sentences to cite."}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":28191,"output":350,"cacheRead":3195,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00302605,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432382773},"fd31811a9bbdb369b4747febe029ee95b55b82aa3fc172996186d8227c000f85":{"response":{"text":"c127 has no project sentences, only a keyword list and an IT support background, so I can't evidence it. I'll try one reformulation aimed at Postgres and Python project work before requesting approval.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01U9uB7VozEKHFyu7KdNdzxL","name":"search_candidates","input":{"reason":"c127 is keyword-only with no project evidence; reformulate toward project-level Postgres migration and Python service ownership to find a fifth evidenced candidate.","query":"owned a Python backend service backed by PostgreSQL, query plan tuning and index migration, shipped semantic search endpoints to production","mode":"semantic","k":12}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":30693,"output":507,"cacheRead":3195,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00335475,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432386079},"39d758eafc287ce96dc1516b9a3083cc876575846ff6fb89328b077fcc8c6add":{"response":{"text":"Four candidates are fully evidenced on all three must-haves. The remaining searches only surfaced keyword-only or non-matching profiles, so I'll request approval for these four.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01GzdGKrog6eWWKPhdwrnbvB","name":"request_hr_approval","input":{"reason":"Four candidates are fully evidenced on all must-haves with verbatim project sentences; the fifth slot has no evidenced candidate after two reformulations, so I'm requesting approval for the four.","candidate_ids":["c049","c156","c066","c002"],"rationale":"Shortlist of four, below the target of five. Evidence (verbatim project sentences) for vector search, Python and Postgres: c049 (Aditya Novak), c156 (Ishita Nguyen), c066 (Elena Bose), c002 (Hana Tanaka). All four also show nice-to-have evidence for embeddings pipeline and API design. c127 (Pooja Desai) has keyword-only profile text with no project sentences and was not evidenced. Other profiles surfaced by the reformulated searches had no must-have evidence. Identity fields were not used."}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":34191,"output":539,"cacheRead":3195,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00372055,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432389673},"a2c7f462cc6d4438b3be6dafb4c910ef2916a8540e23af08f546bcd1eeb0d9c6":{"response":{"text":"All four are fully evidenced on the three must-haves, so the assessment should target the nice-to-have gaps. Three of them have only partial API design evidence (c049, c156, c002), so I'll generate questions on n2 for those three. c066's nice-to-haves were both strong, so I'll leave it out.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_011rvepLoEP2npE7RkenNWNT","name":"create_assessment","input":{"reason":"All four HR-approved candidates have verified must-haves; the open questions are the nice-to-haves, and API design is only partially evidenced for c049, c156 and c002, so I'm targeting n2 for those three and skipping c066, whose nice-to-haves were both strong.","candidates":[{"candidate_id":"c049","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]},{"candidate_id":"c156","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]},{"candidate_id":"c002","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":36711,"output":687,"cacheRead":3040,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.004045000000000001,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432396646},"83bb8835aa984de0d4e0905355129b40aff4983d13c0bde2db135ddefeadd5f6":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"q-code-contract\",\"type\":\"code\",\"prompt\":\"Implement solve(input) in JavaScript. The input is a JSON string with two keys: \\\"required\\\" (an array of field names) and \\\"payload\\\" (an object). Return \\\"valid\\\" if every required field is present in the payload. Otherwise return \\\"missing:\\\" followed by the missing field names sorted alphabetically and joined with commas. Extra payload fields are allowed.\",\"skills\":[\"REST API contract ownership\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":true,\"tests\":[{\"input\":\"{\\\"required\\\":[\\\"id\\\",\\\"name\\\"],\\\"payload\\\":{\\\"id\\\":1,\\\"name\\\":\\\"a\\\"}}\",\"expected\":\"valid\"},{\"input\":\"{\\\"required\\\":[\\\"id\\\",\\\"name\\\"],\\\"payload\\\":{\\\"id\\\":1}}\",\"expected\":\"missing:name\"},{\"input\":\"{\\\"required\\\":[\\\"id\\\",\\\"name\\\"],\\\"payload\\\":{}}\",\"expected\":\"missing:id,name\"},{\"input\":\"{\\\"required\\\":[\\\"id\\\"],\\\"payload\\\":{\\\"id\\\":1,\\\"extra\\\":true}}\",\"expected\":\"valid\"},{\"input\":\"{\\\"required\\\":[],\\\"payload\\\":{}}\",\"expected\":\"valid\"}],\"estimatedMinutes\":12,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-009\"]},{\"id\":\"q-fastapi-services\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"Describe how you would structure a FastAPI application with separate ingestion and query services. Cover how you would validate incoming request bodies, where the service boundaries sit, and how you would handle long-running ingestion without blocking query endpoints. Name specific FastAPI or Python features you would use and explain why.\",\"skills\":[\"FastAPI\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"rubric\":\"Score out of 4. +1 for a clear separation of ingestion and query into distinct routers or services with defined boundaries. +1 for request and response validation using Pydantic models with typed schemas. +1 for a concrete approach to long-running ingestion, such as background tasks, a task queue, or async I/O, that keeps query endpoints responsive. +1 for naming accurate FastAPI/Python details (for example APIRouter, Depends, BackgroundTasks, async def) with a sound reason for each. Deduct points for vague answers with no specific mechanisms.\",\"estimatedMinutes\":8,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-003\"]},{\"id\":\"q-contract-breaking\",\"type\":\"mcq\",\"prompt\":\"You own a public REST API consumed by three client teams. A field must be renamed for a new requirement. Which approach best reflects strong REST contract ownership?\",\"skills\":[\"REST API contract ownership\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"options\":[\"Rename the field immediately and notify clients after deployment\",\"Add the new field alongside the old one, version the change, document the deprecation, and remove the old field after clients migrate\",\"Keep the old field forever without documentation so no client breaks\",\"Rename the field and update only the client team that requested it\"],\"answerKey\":\"Add the new field alongside the old one, version the change, document the deprecation, and remove the old field after clients migrate\",\"estimatedMinutes\":4,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-009\"]}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":259,"output":1858,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":698},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0010421500000000002,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432405570},"972e3c7d738fbcdfaab6672c73d7f4f10816e082320a7760c8f38bdc841873b0":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"q1\",\"type\":\"code\",\"prompt\":\"Write a JavaScript function solve(input) where input is a string of newline-separated route definitions, each in the form 'METHOD /path'. A route is valid if METHOD is one of GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, or DELETE, and the path starts with '/', has no trailing slash unless it is exactly '/', and contains no spaces. Return the string 'OK' if every route is valid. Otherwise return the first invalid line, trimmed, exactly as it appeared.\",\"skills\":[\"REST API design\"],\"weight\":2,\"required\":true,\"tests\":[{\"input\":\"GET /users\\\\nPOST /users\",\"expected\":\"OK\"},{\"input\":\"GET /users/\\\\nPOST /users\",\"expected\":\"GET /users/\"},{\"input\":\"FETCH /users\",\"expected\":\"FETCH /users\"},{\"input\":\"DELETE /users/1\\\\nPATCH /users/1\",\"expected\":\"OK\"},{\"input\":\"GET users\",\"expected\":\"GET users\"}],\"estimatedMinutes\":15,\"claimIds\":[\"skill-003\"]},{\"id\":\"q2\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"Describe how you would structure a FastAPI service with one ingestion endpoint and one query endpoint. Cover the request and response models, how you validate input, and how the two endpoints share or separate storage. Include one example of a route signature.\",\"skills\":[\"FastAPI\"],\"weight\":2,\"required\":false,\"rubric\":\"Score out of 5. 1 point: uses FastAPI route decorators with typed path, query, or body parameters. 1 point: names Pydantic models for request and response with validation (e.g., field constraints or response_model). 1 point: separates ingestion (write) and query (read) concerns, such as distinct routes, service layer, or storage access. 1 point: gives a concrete, syntactically plausible route signature. 1 point: addresses at least one operational concern such as error handling (HTTPException), background processing, or pagination. Award 0 for a generic answer with no FastAPI-specific detail.\",\"estimatedMinutes\":10,\"claimIds\":[\"skill-006\"]},{\"id\":\"q3\",\"type\":\"mcq\",\"prompt\":\"In FastAPI, which mechanism validates an incoming JSON request body against a declared schema before the route handler runs?\",\"skills\":[\"FastAPI\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"options\":[\"Pydantic models used as type hints on the handler parameter\",\"Calling json.loads inside the handler body\",\"Attaching a SQLAlchemy ORM model to the route decorator\",\"Wrapping the app in a Flask-WTF form object\"],\"answerKey\":\"Pydantic models used as type hints on the handler parameter\",\"estimatedMinutes\":3,\"claimIds\":[\"skill-006\"]}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":250,"output":1921,"cacheRead":698,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00099248,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432414773},"3ec2824ed6a7d42852679e2a2e6737b99de42cc10ed9ae044769c426eea943ec":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"q-code-pg-index\",\"type\":\"code\",\"prompt\":\"Write a JavaScript function solve(input) where input is a JSON string with the shape {\\\"indexes\\\":[\\\"table.column\\\",...],\\\"queries\\\":[{\\\"table\\\":\\\"...\\\",\\\"column\\\":\\\"...\\\"},...]}. Each query filters on one table column. A query is served by an index only if \\\"table.column\\\" appears in indexes. Return, as a string, the number of queries that have no matching index and would therefore require a sequential scan.\",\"skills\":[\"PostgreSQL query tuning\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":true,\"answerKey\":\"Counts queries whose table.column is not in the indexes list and returns the count as a string.\",\"tests\":[{\"input\":\"{\\\"indexes\\\":[\\\"users.email\\\"],\\\"queries\\\":[{\\\"table\\\":\\\"users\\\",\\\"column\\\":\\\"email\\\"}]}\",\"expected\":\"0\"},{\"input\":\"{\\\"indexes\\\":[],\\\"queries\\\":[{\\\"table\\\":\\\"orders\\\",\\\"column\\\":\\\"status\\\"},{\\\"table\\\":\\\"orders\\\",\\\"column\\\":\\\"status\\\"}]}\",\"expected\":\"2\"},{\"input\":\"{\\\"indexes\\\":[\\\"users.email\\\",\\\"orders.status\\\"],\\\"queries\\\":[{\\\"table\\\":\\\"users\\\",\\\"column\\\":\\\"id\\\"},{\\\"table\\\":\\\"orders\\\",\\\"column\\\":\\\"status\\\"}]}\",\"expected\":\"1\"},{\"input\":\"{\\\"indexes\\\":[],\\\"queries\\\":[]}\",\"expected\":\"0\"}],\"estimatedMinutes\":15,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-002\"]},{\"id\":\"q-mcq-fastapi-pydantic\",\"type\":\"mcq\",\"prompt\":\"In FastAPI, what is the main purpose of declaring request bodies with Pydantic models?\",\"skills\":[\"FastAPI\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"options\":[\"To automatically validate and parse incoming request data and generate OpenAPI schema\",\"To compile Python endpoints into native machine code for speed\",\"To manage PostgreSQL connection pooling\",\"To replace the ASGI server in production deployments\"],\"answerKey\":\"To automatically validate and parse incoming request data and generate OpenAPI schema\",\"estimatedMinutes\":5,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-003\"]},{\"id\":\"q-short-fastapi-di\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"Describe how you would use FastAPI dependency injection to share a database session across endpoints while ensuring it is closed after each request. Include a brief code sketch or clear description.\",\"skills\":[\"FastAPI\"],\"weight\":1,\"required\":false,\"rubric\":\"Full credit (2 points): Uses Depends with a generator function that yields a session and closes it in a finally or after-yield block; mentions applying the dependency to multiple routes. Partial credit (1 point): Identifies Depends or dependency injection but omits the cleanup step or gives an unclear lifecycle. No credit (0 points): Describes global session use without request-scoped handling or does not mention FastAPI dependencies.\",\"estimatedMinutes\":10,\"claimIds\":[\"claim-003\"]}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":266,"output":1933,"cacheRead":698,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00100008,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432423493},"22a15d60c2a9ae9792db81d2f4772d34302833c903f9afb0028ed742d19c6b6c":{"response":{"text":"c066 is HR-approved but has no assessment yet, and the exit condition requires one. I'll generate it now, then send all four in one batch.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_017xXnZCXzFdN5Cx9CytPofh","name":"create_assessment","input":{"reason":"c066 is HR-approved but was left out of the last assessment batch; the exit condition requires every approved candidate to be assessed. Its API design cite was implied, so I'm asking about n2 for a verifiable check.","candidates":[{"candidate_id":"c066","unverified_requirement_ids":["n2"]}]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":40159,"output":416,"cacheRead":3040,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0042543,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432426239},"ced3b0b298996902d6f617d058e3f82078305a3e65625bf1781ce2811018fe4b":{"response":{"text":"{\"items\":[{\"id\":\"q1\",\"type\":\"mcq\",\"prompt\":\"A team moved search from Elasticsearch to pgvector in PostgreSQL. Which trade-off is the most important to verify before claiming the migration was a like-for-like replacement?\",\"skills\":[\"pgvector\"],\"required\":false,\"answerKey\":\"Whether filtered vector queries and keyword/relevance scoring still meet latency and recall targets after moving to PostgreSQL\",\"options\":[\"Whether the team kept the same Kibana dashboards\",\"Whether filtered vector queries and keyword/relevance scoring still meet latency and recall targets after moving to PostgreSQL\",\"Whether the number of Elasticsearch shards matches the number of PostgreSQL tables\",\"Whether the application language changed from Java to Python\"],\"estimatedMinutes\":3,\"claimIds\":[\"skill-001\"]},{\"id\":\"q2\",\"type\":\"short\",\"prompt\":\"You built HNSW indexes for vector retrieval. Explain how the ef_construction, M, and ef_search parameters affect index build time, memory use, and recall, and how you would tune them for a workload with 10 million 768-dimension vectors and a p95 latency target of 50 ms.\",\"skills\":[\"HNSW indexing\"],\"required\":true,\"rubric\":\"Score out of 4. 1 point: M controls graph connectivity, raising it improves recall but increases memory and build time. 1 point: ef_construction controls build-time candidate list size, raising it improves graph quality but slows the build. 1 point: ef_search controls query-time candidate list size and is the main recall/latency knob, tunable without rebuilding. 1 point: gives a concrete tuning approach for the stated scale and latency target, such as sweeping ef_search against measured recall and p95 latency, with memory estimated from vector size and M. Deduct points for vague answers that mix up parameters.\",\"estimatedMinutes\":8,\"claimIds\":[\"skill-002\"]},{\"id\":\"q3\",\"type\":\"code\",\"prompt\":\"Implement solve(input) in JavaScript. The input is a JSON string with fields k (integer), query (number array), and vectors (array of number arrays). Return a JSON string of the indices of the k vectors with highest cosine similarity to the query, sorted by descending similarity. Break ties by lower index. Treat a zero-length-norm vector as similarity 0. This is a brute-force baseline for checking approximate HNSW recall.\",\"skills\":[\"HNSW indexing\"],\"required\":false,\"tests\":[{\"input\":\"{\\\"k\\\":2,\\\"query\\\":[1,0],\\\"vectors\\\":[[1,0],[0,1],[0.7,0.7]]}\",\"expected\":\"[0,2]\"},{\"input\":\"{\\\"k\\\":1,\\\"query\\\":[0,1],\\\"vectors\\\":[[1,0],[0,2],[0,1]]}\",\"expected\":\"[1]\"},{\"input\":\"{\\\"k\\\":3,\\\"query\\\":[1,1],\\\"vectors\\\":[[1,1],[2,2],[1,-1]]}\",\"expected\":\"[0,1,2]\"},{\"input\":\"{\\\"k\\\":2,\\\"query\\\":[3,4],\\\"vectors\\\":[[4,3],[0,0],[-3,-4]]}\",\"expected\":\"[0,1]\"}],\"estimatedMinutes\":15,\"claimIds\":[\"skill-002\"]}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":260,"output":2127,"cacheRead":698,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00109648,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432436308},"8e44d3cb077cc389ad7be6a9893202862b1483daac946d61f6941de883926628":{"response":{"text":"All four approved candidates now have assessments created. Next I'll send them out.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01GKPoSHjFcUWcVD6n7UfKFQ","name":"send_assessment","input":{"reason":"All four HR-approved candidates (c049, c156, c066, c002) now have generated assessments, so I'm sending them in one batch.","candidate_ids":["c049","c156","c066","c002"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":42784,"output":158,"cacheRead":3040,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0043878,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432437373},"7b1acff9c9a04588b58ffaaab0fffe16b8008b9755b1243f386f2644dd3a1bbb":{"response":{"text":"The assessments are sent, so the next step is to collect the candidates' answers.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01YXDwLiJu6FdvKRqDwhFW9R","name":"collect_responses","input":{"reason":"Assessments were sent to all four HR-approved candidates; collect their answers before running code checks and grading.","candidate_ids":["c049","c156","c066","c002"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":45314,"output":149,"cacheRead":3040,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0046363,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432438552},"b7ad20175d4852f9a843b69884842d102e3bf3c278922b5f6a5e4b6aeecdc4e4":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"id\":\"q-code-contract\",\"answer\":\"function solve(input) {\\n  const { required, payload } = JSON.parse(input);\\n  const missing = required.filter(f => !payload[f]).sort();\\n  return missing.length === 0 ? \\\"valid\\\" : \\\"missing:\\\" + missing.join(\\\",\\\");\\n}\"},{\"id\":\"q-fastapi-services\",\"answer\":\"I'd split this into two FastAPI apps or two routers behind one gateway: an ingestion service that owns writes to the index, and a query service that only reads. Request bodies get Pydantic v2 models with typed fields, Field constraints, and validators, so bad input is rejected with a 422 before it touches business logic. For boundaries, the query service talks to the index (pgvector or FAISS) through a small repository layer, and ingestion publishes or writes through the same layer so the schema lives in one place. For long-running ingestion, I'd accept the job with a 202 response and a job id, then run the work in background workers using asyncio tasks or a task queue, with BackgroundTasks for small jobs. Heavy CPU work like embedding goes into a thread or process pool via run_in_executor so the event loop stays free for query endpoints. Status is polled through a separate jobs endpoint. Dependency injection through Depends handles shared clients and config, and lifespan handlers manage the index load and warm caches at startup.\"},{\"id\":\"q-contract-breaking\",\"answer\":\"Add the new field alongside the old one, version the change, document the deprecation, and remove the old field after clients migrate\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":933,"output":927,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0005568,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432443341},"6203e74adafa149d96b767f7549db85ad5fba8c5356fe516a663148d953addec":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"id\":\"q1\",\"answer\":\"function solve(input) {\\n  const re = /^(GET|POST|PUT|PATCH|DELETE) (\\\\/\\\\S*)$/;\\n  for (const raw of input.split('\\\\n')) {\\n    const line = raw.trim();\\n    if (line === '') continue;\\n    const m = re.exec(line);\\n    if (!m) return line;\\n    const path = m[2];\\n    if (path !== '/' && path.endsWith('/')) return line;\\n  }\\n  return 'OK';\\n}\"},{\"id\":\"q2\",\"answer\":\"I would split the service into a thin router layer, a Pydantic schema layer, and a storage layer behind a small repository interface, so the endpoints stay simple and testable.\\n\\nModels: Define IngestRequest with typed fields (for example id: str, text: str with min and max length constraints, and metadata: dict[str, str] capped in size) and an IngestResponse with the stored id and a status. Define QueryRequest with query: str, top_k: int bounded between 1 and 100, and an optional filter. Define QueryResponse as a list of hits, each with id, score, and a payload. Using response_model on the decorator keeps outgoing data consistent with the declared schema.\\n\\nValidation: Pydantic handles type coercion and constraint checks before the handler runs, so invalid bodies get a 422 without touching business logic. I would add field validators for semantic rules, such as rejecting empty text after stripping whitespace, and keep any cross-field rules in model validators. Dependencies handle things that need the request context, such as an API key check.\\n\\nStorage: Both endpoints share one repository object created at startup through the lifespan handler and injected with Depends. The ingestion path writes the row and its embedding in a single transaction, then updates the index. The query path only reads. For pgvector I would keep the vector column and HNSW index in the same Postgres table so writes are atomic; for FAISS I would keep the index in memory with a background worker that batches adds and persists snapshots. The ingestion service and query service can run as separate processes that share the same database or index file, so read traffic can scale independently of writes. Blocking embedding calls run in a thread pool or a dedicated async worker so the event loop is not stalled.\\n\\nExample route signature:\\n\\n@router.post('/ingest', response_model=IngestResponse, status_code=202)\\nasync def ingest(body: IngestRequest, repo: Repo = Depends(get_repo)) -> IngestResponse:\\n    ...\\n\\nThe query endpoint would follow the same pattern with @router.post('/query', response_model=QueryResponse).\"},{\"id\":\"q3\",\"answer\":\"Pydantic models used as type hints on the handler parameter\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":894,"output":1590,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0008844,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432451437},"c183d9d2a24eabfcad8d7d5c7afb430c997958b4160505fe536f95076799f9e9":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"id\":\"q1\",\"answer\":\"Whether filtered vector queries and keyword/relevance scoring still meet latency and recall targets after moving to PostgreSQL\"},{\"id\":\"q2\",\"answer\":\"In HNSW, M sets the number of bidirectional links per node in the graph. Higher M improves recall and search quality, especially at high dimension, but increases memory roughly linearly with M and makes index build slower. ef_construction sets the candidate list size while inserting vectors. Higher values produce a better-connected graph and higher recall at a given search effort, but build time grows substantially and the build cost scales with the candidate list. ef_search sets the candidate list size at query time. It has no effect on index size or build time. It is the main runtime knob: raising it improves recall and increases query latency, roughly in proportion to the number of nodes visited. For 10 million 768-dimension float32 vectors, the raw vectors alone are about 30 GB, so memory planning must include the graph overhead, which grows with M. I would start with M around 16 to 32, ef_construction around 100 to 200, and then sweep ef_search on a labelled query set that has exact brute-force ground truth. I would pick the smallest ef_search that reaches the target recall, for example 0.95 recall@10, and verify that p95 latency stays under 50 ms under realistic concurrency. If recall is short at acceptable ef_search, I would increase M or ef_construction and rebuild, accepting the extra memory and build time. I would also consider scalar or product quantization, or partitioning, if memory is the binding constraint. Filtered queries need separate measurement, because restrictive filters can reduce effective recall and latency.\"},{\"id\":\"q3\",\"answer\":\"function solve(input) {\\n  const { k, query, vectors } = JSON.parse(input);\\n  const qNorm = Math.sqrt(query.reduce((s, x) => s + x * x, 0));\\n  const scored = vectors.map((v, i) => {\\n    let dot = 0;\\n    let vNormSq = 0;\\n    const len = Math.min(v.length, query.length);\\n    for (let j = 0; j < len; j++) dot += v[j] * query[j];\\n    for (const x of v) vNormSq += x * x;\\n    const vNorm = Math.sqrt(vNormSq);\\n    const sim = (qNorm === 0 || vNorm === 0) ? 0 : dot / (qNorm * vNorm);\\n    return { i, sim };\\n  });\\n  scored.sort((a, b) => (b.sim - a.sim) || (a.i - b.i));\\n  return JSON.stringify(scored.slice(0, k).map(s => s.i));\\n}\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":934,"output":1460,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0008234,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432458404},"83537437675f770c7bea9c137d610e8ec1fe2ffcb71b86e995b4998e9973da49":{"response":{"text":"{\"answers\":[{\"id\":\"q-code-pg-index\",\"answer\":\"function solve(input) {\\n  const data = JSON.parse(input);\\n  const indexes = new Set(data.indexes);\\n  let count = 0;\\n  for (const q of data.queries) {\\n    if (!indexes.has(q.column)) {\\n      count++;\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return String(count);\\n}\"},{\"id\":\"q-mcq-fastapi-pydantic\",\"answer\":\"To automatically validate and parse incoming request data and generate OpenAPI schema\"},{\"id\":\"q-short-fastapi-di\",\"answer\":\"I'd create a session factory with SQLAlchemy and wrap it in a dependency function so FastAPI can inject it into endpoints. Something like:\\n\\ndef get_db():\\n    db = SessionLocal()\\n    yield db\\n    db.close()\\n\\n@app.get('/items')\\ndef list_items(db: Session = Depends(get_db)):\\n    return db.query(Item).all()\\n\\nUsing yield means FastAPI runs the code after the yield once the request finishes, so the session gets closed. Endpoints just declare the Depends(get_db) parameter and reuse the same pattern for other routes. I haven't really worried about commit or rollback handling in this sketch.\"}]}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":850,"output":797,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0004835,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432462511},"28744b0b26e2296f60d2389e7481dbd8075d55aeb17e9d9be1a3864e2d137029":{"response":{"text":"Responses are in, and two of the four include code answers. Next I'll run the code answers against the hidden tests before grading.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01Sd73Hickkxm73teg8YJLWM","name":"run_code","input":{"reason":"Responses include JavaScript code answers from all four candidates; run them against the hidden tests before grading so code scores rest on execution, not just rubric.","candidate_ids":["c049","c156","c066","c002"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":48329,"output":174,"cacheRead":3040,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0049503,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432464000},"6c3b0efa49406723985f9ddeb835c6137532c1bb29c84ef57b85e2a005af0642":{"response":{"text":"","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01Y7NEoniAqGGQWe4Uobay76","name":"grade_answer","input":{"reason":"Code tests have run (c049 timed out 0/5, c156 2/5, c066 4/4, c002 2/4); grade all four against the rubric, combined with these test results.","candidate_ids":["c049","c156","c066","c002"]}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":50677,"output":147,"cacheRead":3040,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0051716,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432466718},"a77c0f352c0232e57df5d980c413466f7c87ce57c7ea19fc90827016f454a7c1":{"response":{"text":"{\"score\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Covers all four rubric points. It separates ingestion and query into distinct services or routers with a shared repository boundary. It uses Pydantic v2 models with Field constraints and validators, and explains the 422 rejection. For long-running ingestion it gives a concrete design: a 202 response with a job id, asyncio tasks or a task queue, BackgroundTasks for small jobs, run_in_executor for CPU-bound embedding, and a polling endpoint. It names Depends, lifespan, and async, with reasons for each. Minor gaps: response models are not explicitly covered, and it does not note that BackgroundTasks shares the process with query traffic, which is why it should be limited to small jobs.\",\"confidence\":0.85}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":846,"output":509,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0003391,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432469658},"f1a7e4ad2f7f0fdd5cd5df6e4391d24fe9e6953825f9b684496e71660dd9cd2e":{"response":{"text":"{\"score\":1.0,\"reason\":\"The answer meets all five rubric criteria. It uses FastAPI route decorators with typed body parameters (@router.post with body: IngestRequest). It names Pydantic request and response models with field constraints (length bounds, top_k between 1 and 100) and uses response_model. It separates write and read concerns through distinct routes, a shared repository layer, and the option of separate ingestion and query processes. It gives a concrete, plausible route signature with status_code and Depends. It addresses operational concerns, including background batching for FAISS, a thread pool for blocking embedding calls, and 202 status for ingestion. It does not explicitly mention HTTPException or pagination, but background processing alone satisfies the criterion.\",\"confidence\":0.9}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":1185,"output":504,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0003705,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432473854},"3a70bfc2225dee9ecdc675511d1206da191e905c4230a5ff47287c822588ce9d":{"response":{"text":"{\"score\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Covers all four rubric points accurately. M is correctly described as controlling graph connectivity, with higher values improving recall at the cost of memory and build time. ef_construction is correctly described as the build-time candidate list size that improves graph quality but slows the build. ef_search is correctly identified as the query-time recall/latency knob, with no effect on index size or build time. The tuning approach is concrete for the stated scale: it computes raw vector memory (~30 GB for 10M x 768 float32), sweeps ef_search against brute-force ground truth, picks the smallest ef_search meeting a recall target, and checks p95 latency under concurrency. Minor gap: graph memory is noted as growing with M but no explicit estimate formula is given, and it does not state directly that ef_search is tunable without rebuilding, though this is implied.\",\"confidence\":0.9}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":1006,"output":520,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00036060000000000004,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432476730},"5bb8ecb9a72abdfcabb7670929c460cedc9c379b6616cc291a3995287ada8a9d":{"response":{"text":"{\"score\":1.0,\"reason\":\"The answer uses Depends with a generator function (get_db) that yields a SessionLocal session and calls db.close() in the after-yield block, which the rubric accepts. It also says endpoints declare the Depends(get_db) parameter and that the same pattern applies to other routes, meeting the multi-route requirement. The missing commit/rollback handling is not part of the rubric, so it does not reduce the score.\",\"confidence\":0.92}","toolUses":[],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"input":656,"output":354,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.00024260000000000002,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432478872},"70df553b42a5740b9ef8469c818bd4c54d93476e5ff08ef3f9467589012fd61b":{"response":{"text":"c049's grade is flagged: its code answer timed out with confidence 0, so the code part is unscored. I'll regrade c049 once; if it stays flagged, it goes to HR.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_01Uchukup52xUFWK1sdgnBgZ","name":"grade_answer","input":{"reason":"c049's code answer timed out (0/5, confidence 0), so the rubric and tests disagree and the grade is flagged; regrade once for c049 only.","candidate_ids":["c049"],"regrade":true}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":54429,"output":250,"cacheRead":3040,"cacheWrite":0},"model":"claude-haiku-5-5"},"costUsd":0.0055983000000000005,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ts":1793432480512},"5bafe42870ed5db27c4594817bf6fab2a4907421e7b63fec513d89db23c739fd":{"response":{"text":"c049 is still flagged after the regrade, so per the rules it goes to HR. All four approved candidates now have grades, which meets the ASSESS exit condition, so I'm advancing to AUDIT.","toolUses":[{"id":"toolu_011d3hMPLjVAT4WETQ7ybVwh","name":"advance_phase","input":{"reason":"All four HR-approved candidates have grades (c049 flagged after one regrade and left for HR); ASSESS exit condition is met."}}],"stopReason":"tool_use","usage":{"input":57152,"output":25